window.CET_INDEX = {"schema":"cet-index/1","sets":[{"id":"cet4-mock-1","level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":1,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第1套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet4-mock-1.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet4-mock-2","level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":2,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第2套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet4-mock-2.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet4-mock-3","level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":3,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第3套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet4-mock-3.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet4-mock-4","level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":4,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第4套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet4-mock-4.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet6-mock-1","level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":1,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第1套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet6-mock-1.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet6-mock-2","level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":2,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第2套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet6-mock-2.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet6-mock-3","level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":3,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第3套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet6-mock-3.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}},{"id":"cet6-mock-4","level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":4,"mock":true,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第4套","writingTitle":"For this part, you are allowed 30 minute","file":"cet6-mock-4.json","counts":{"reading":30,"writing":1,"translation":1}}]};
window.CET_BANK = {"cet4-mock-1":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":1,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第1套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"Baking bread at home looks simple, yet it punishes anyone in a hurry. Beginners usually fail not for lack of skill but for lack of [[26]].\nThe ingredients are plain: flour, water, salt and a little yeast. First [[27]] the flour and water with a wooden spoon until no dry patches remain, then move the mass onto a clean surface.\nKneading comes next. For ten minutes you push the dough away with the heel of your hand, fold it back and repeat. These folds [[28]] the gluten, the elastic web that traps gas.\nThen comes fermentation. Cover the bowl and leave it in a warm corner. Yeast cells feed on sugars in the flour and [[29]] carbon dioxide; the gas stays inside the web, and the dough slowly doubles. How long this takes will [[30]] on room temperature.\nOnce it has risen, press the dough down, shape it and let it rest. This second rise is brief, yet [[31]] to the final texture: a loaf that skips it comes out heavy.\nBaking is the last test. A very hot oven will [[32]] a firm shell on the outside, and that shell keeps the loaf from collapsing. Do not slice it at once: steam is still moving through the centre, and a knife will [[33]] the crumb into a sticky mass.\nWait at least half an hour before cutting, [[34]] on a wire rack so air reaches the base. The reward is the smell that fills the kitchen and the quiet [[35]] of making something good from plain ingredients.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"combine"},{"k":"B","t":"depend"},{"k":"C","t":"develop"},{"k":"D","t":"essential"},{"k":"E","t":"flavour"},{"k":"F","t":"form"},{"k":"G","t":"gradual"},{"k":"H","t":"ideally"},{"k":"I","t":"patience"},{"k":"J","t":"pride"},{"k":"K","t":"rarely"},{"k":"L","t":"reduce"},{"k":"M","t":"release"},{"k":"N","t":"steady"},{"k":"O","t":"turn"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) patience。第 1 段第 2 句说初学者失败不是因为缺少技巧，而是 for lack of 某物，而第 1 句已点明这个流程会惩罚 in a hurry 的人，patience 正与「匆忙」相对，且 lack of 后需要名词。E) flavour 与 J) pride 虽也是名词，但与「缺少技巧」并列时语义不通。"},{"n":27,"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A) combine。第 2 段第 2 句以 First 引出祈使句，空格需动词原形；combine the flour and water 与后面 until no dry patches remain（不再有干粉）呼应，说明是把两者混合。L) reduce 与 O) turn 都不能直接接 the flour and water 表示「混合」。"},{"n":28,"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C) develop。第 3 段第 3 句主语 These folds 是复数，谓语需动词原形，develop the gluten 是固定搭配，指反复折叠使面筋形成。G) gradual 与 N) steady 是形容词，不能作谓语。"},{"n":29,"answer":"M","explanation":"答案 M) release。第 4 段第 3 句主语 Yeast cells 为复数，与并列的 feed 时态一致，需动词原形；release carbon dioxide 指酵母释放二氧化碳，后文 the gas 即回指它。L) reduce 与二氧化碳搭配在语义上说不通。"},{"n":30,"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B) depend。第 4 段最后一句 will 后接动词原形，depend on 为固定搭配，说明发酵时间长短取决于室温。A) combine 不能与 on 搭配表示「取决于」。"},{"n":31,"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D) essential。第 5 段第 2 句 yet 表转折，brief 为形容词，空格后接 to the final texture，需能与 to 搭配的形容词，essential to 意为「对……必不可少」。G) gradual 与 N) steady 都不能接 to 表达这种关系。"},{"n":32,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) form。第 6 段第 2 句 will 后接动词原形，高温会在表面 form a firm shell，与后文 that shell 形成呼应。O) turn 表示「变成」时须接 into，此处宾语是 a firm shell，搭配不成立。"},{"n":33,"answer":"O","explanation":"答案 O) turn。第 6 段最后一句 will 后接动词原形，turn sth into sth 为固定搭配，指过早下刀会把面包心弄得黏糊。L) reduce 虽也可接 into，但意为「使变成某种更差的状态」时多用于抽象语境，与 a sticky mass 搭配不当。"},{"n":34,"answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H) ideally。第 7 段第 1 句主句 Wait at least half an hour 结构已完整，空格后是 on a wire rack 这一地点状语，需用副词修饰整个短语，ideally 表示「最好」。K) rarely 语义相反，与随后的建议矛盾。"},{"n":35,"answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J) pride。第 7 段第 2 句 the quiet ___ of making something 中 of 短语修饰空格，需名词；pride 与前半句 the reward 呼应，指亲手做成东西的自豪。E) flavour 不能与 of making 搭配，且与 quiet 修饰关系不合。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Ask someone to describe a university library and the answer is usually a quiet room lined with shelves. That picture is out of date. Across Northfield and dozens of similar institutions, the library has become the busiest building on campus, and very little of the activity involves borrowing a printed book. The change did not happen overnight, and no single committee planned it. It came from several pressures arriving at the same time: falling demand for print, rising demand for space, and a new understanding of how students actually study, alone and in groups, at every hour of the day."},{"label":"B","text":"The most visible change is physical. Many libraries have moved the bulk of their print collections into compact shelving in the basement or into a shared storage building, which frees whole floors for people rather than paper. At Northfield, two floors that once held bound periodicals now contain long tables with power sockets, soft chairs, and small rooms that can be booked online. A student who wants a single quiet desk can still find one, but that desk may now sit beside a glass wall through which a seminar is visible. Visitors often remark that the building feels larger, although no wall has been moved."},{"label":"C","text":"Behind the scenes, the collection itself has changed shape. The library now spends a larger share of its budget on electronic journals, databases and e-books than on printed volumes, and students reach most of these from a laptop in a dormitory at midnight. Print circulation has fallen for fifteen years without a pause. Reference works that once filled shelves are consulted online, and one subscription can serve several thousand readers at the same moment. Librarians point out that this shift saves space but not money, since electronic licences are renewed every year and rise in price faster than inflation."},{"label":"D","text":"The new floors are designed for collaboration, a word that would have puzzled librarians a generation ago. Movable tables let four students form a group in seconds; whiteboards cover entire walls; and glass partitions keep discussion from disturbing readers next door. Staff report that demand for group space far exceeds supply, especially in the weeks before examinations. Some buildings now label zones by noise level rather than by subject, so a student can choose silence, conversation or something in between, and the choice is made before the books are opened rather than after a complaint."},{"label":"E","text":"A newer addition is the makerspace, usually tucked into a corner of the ground floor. It may contain 3D printers, a laser cutter, sewing machines, audio booths and software for editing video. The equipment is expensive to buy and to maintain, and few students use it every week, yet it has become a symbol of what the library now claims to offer: not only texts to read but tools to make things with. Supporters argue that a shared workshop removes a barrier for students who could never afford such machines at home, and that the skills learned there travel well beyond the campus."},{"label":"F","text":"The people who work there have changed too. Reference desks, where a librarian once answered factual questions, have largely disappeared, because most of those questions can now be settled by a search engine in seconds. In their place are appointments for research advice, help with data analysis, and guidance on copyright and open access. One librarian described the shift as moving from answering questions to teaching people how to ask better ones, which takes longer, cannot be done from behind a desk, and is much harder to count at the end of a year."},{"label":"G","text":"Not everyone is pleased. Students who need absolute quiet complain that the building has become a social centre and that the few silent rooms are always full. Maintenance costs have risen sharply: machines break, furniture wears out, and a makerspace needs trained staff on duty whenever it is open. Librarians also worry about the message sent when a university invests in cafes and game rooms while the budget for new books shrinks. The criticism is fair, and it has forced several libraries to publish their spending figures and to explain publicly why a smaller book budget is not the same as a smaller collection."},{"label":"H","text":"Measuring success is harder than it sounds. Gate counts once told the whole story, but a student who enters to meet a friend and never opens a book counts exactly the same as one who spends six hours on a thesis. Northfield now combines several indicators: bookings of group rooms, downloads of electronic resources, attendance at workshops, and short surveys sent twice a year. None of these numbers is reliable on its own, and staff admit that they still cannot say precisely what makes a library good, only which parts of it are being used and by whom."},{"label":"I","text":"Equity has become part of the design brief. Extended opening hours matter to students who share a crowded flat or commute long distances, and several libraries now stay open all night during examination periods. Height-adjustable desks, screen readers and quiet pods serve users with disabilities. One administrator noted that the library is often the only indoor space on campus where a student can sit for hours without being expected to buy anything, which makes it especially important for those with the least money and the least comfortable homes."},{"label":"J","text":"What, then, is a library for? The honest answer is that it is no longer defined by what it holds but by what it makes possible. Books remain, and they still matter, but they sit alongside software, workshops, quiet corners and rooms full of conversation. The building has survived because it adapted, and the next decade will probably bring changes that nobody in the current staff meeting can predict. The one constant is the idea of a shared place that belongs to everyone on campus, whether or not they ever borrow a single volume."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"An increasing part of the money spent on collections now buys material that never exists in physical form.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。C 段第 2 句说图书馆把更大比例的预算投向电子期刊、数据库和电子书，而这些资源学生可从宿舍的笔记本电脑上获取，正是「经费越来越多地用于没有实体形态的资料」；A 段只谈整体变化与成因，没有提经费结构。"},{"n":37,"stem":"Some of the newest facilities are costly to run and attract relatively few regular users, yet they are held up as evidence of the library's broader purpose.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。E 段第 3 句指出这些设备购置与维护都很贵、每周使用者不多，后半句 yet it has become a symbol of what the library now claims to offer 与题干「却被当作图书馆更广阔用途的证明」对应；D 段讲的是小组学习区，不涉及设备成本。"},{"n":38,"stem":"People who value silence feel that their needs have been sacrificed to the building's new social role.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。G 段第 2 句说需要绝对安静的学生抱怨图书馆变成了社交中心、仅有的安静房间总被占满，即「认为自己的需求被牺牲」；D 段虽然提到噪音分区，但那是介绍设计思路，并未表达不满。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Taking away rows of shelving has allowed rooms to be given over to seating and bookable study areas without any structural work.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。B 段第 2、3 句说印刷书刊被移入密集书库，腾出整层楼放长桌、软椅和可预约的小房间，末句又说 although no wall has been moved，与题干「没有做任何结构改造」直接对应；A 段只笼统说图书馆变忙。"},{"n":40,"stem":"Librarians increasingly help students frame better questions instead of supplying quick facts.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。F 段第 3 句引用馆员的话，说工作已从 answering questions 转为 teaching people how to ask better ones，与题干「不再提供快速事实，而是帮学生把问题问得更好」同义；C 段讲的是资源形态变化，与馆员角色无关。"},{"n":41,"stem":"Simply counting the people who come through the door fails to show whether they actually study there.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。H 段第 2 句说进馆见朋友、一本书都不翻的人，与写六小时论文的人被算作一样，说明单纯统计入馆人数不能反映真实使用；B 段讲空间改造，与如何衡量无关。"},{"n":42,"stem":"The transformation came about through several separate pressures rather than one deliberate plan.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。A 段第 4、5 句说变化不是一夜之间发生的、也没有哪个委员会统一规划，而是几种压力同时到来（falling demand for print, rising demand for space 等），与题干逐点对应；J 段是全文总结，强调适应能力，不是成因。"},{"n":43,"stem":"Some buildings now organise their floors by how much noise is acceptable rather than by academic field.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。D 段最后一句说一些建筑现在按噪音水平而非按学科划分区域，学生可以选安静、交谈或介于两者之间，与题干「按可接受的噪音程度而非学科分区」几乎逐词对应；G 段谈的是对噪音的抱怨，不是分区方式。"},{"n":44,"stem":"For students with little money, the library may be the only place where they can stay for hours without spending anything.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。I 段最后一句说图书馆常是校园里唯一可以坐上几个小时而不必消费的室内空间，对钱最少、住处最不舒适的学生尤其重要，与题干同义；E 段虽提到共享设备降低门槛，但主语是创客空间而非整座图书馆。"},{"n":45,"stem":"The library has survived because it was willing to change, and further change is expected.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。J 段第 3 句说这座建筑之所以存续是因为它不断调整，未来十年还会出现连现在开会的馆员都预料不到的变化，与题干两点都吻合；A 段只描述已经发生的变化，没有对未来作出判断。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Ten years ago the riverside road in Marlow Bay carried four lanes of traffic and almost no cyclists. Today a wide green strip runs along the water, separated from cars by a low concrete barrier, and on a weekday morning it is crowded. The change followed a decision by the city council to remove one lane of parking and one lane of traffic along a three-kilometre stretch. Officials expected complaints. They received more than they had bargained for, along with something they had not expected: a steady rise in the number of people who cycle to work.\nThe numbers are striking. Cycling trips along the corridor rose from about 900 a day to 3,400 within two years. Shops on the street reported mixed results: cafes and bakeries gained customers, while a hardware store that depended on drivers loading heavy goods lost trade and eventually closed. Traffic on parallel streets increased by roughly eight per cent, and journey times for drivers there lengthened by two to four minutes at peak hours.\nSupporters argue that such figures miss the point. A cyclist who passes a shop ten times a week is more likely to stop than a driver who passes once, and a street with fewer cars is pleasanter to walk along. They also point to safety: collisions involving cyclists on the corridor fell by half after the barrier was installed, and hospital admissions for serious injuries followed the same pattern. For a group that had long been told to ride more carefully, separating bikes from fast traffic felt like an admission that the road itself had been the problem.\nCritics are not persuaded. They note that the corridor serves a wealthy district close to the city centre, where many residents already owned bicycles, and that the same money could have funded bus services in poorer suburbs where car ownership is low but so is access to work. A shopkeepers' association commissioned its own survey and reported that a third of its members had seen turnover fall. The council's own review admitted that the benefits were unevenly distributed, though it insisted that the overall balance was positive.\nWhat the dispute reveals is less about bicycles than about who a street is for. For decades the answer was assumed: cars, with everything else fitted around them. The new lane is an experiment in reversing that order, and like most experiments it produces winners and losers at the same time. The council has promised to review the scheme after five years, and other districts are watching closely. Whether the model travels well to poorer areas, where a bicycle is a cost rather than a lifestyle choice, remains genuinely uncertain.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What did the city council do to create the cycle lane?","options":{"A":"It closed the riverside road to all motor traffic.","B":"It took away a parking lane and a traffic lane along part of the road.","C":"It widened the road by building out over the river bank.","D":"It banned heavy goods vehicles from the whole district."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段第 3 句说议会决定在三公里长的路段上 remove one lane of parking and one lane of traffic，B 项是这句话的同义复述。A 项把它说成禁止一切机动车，属过度推断；C 项「向河岸扩建」原文没有依据。"},{"n":47,"stem":"What happened to the shops on the street after the change?","options":{"A":"Every one of them reported a fall in turnover.","B":"Cafes lost customers while the hardware store gained trade.","C":"Food businesses gained customers while a shop serving drivers closed.","D":"Most of them moved to the parallel streets nearby."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 2 段第 3 句说 cafes and bakeries gained customers，而依赖司机装卸重货的五金店 lost trade and eventually closed，C 项概括准确。A 项与原文相反（原文说 mixed results）；B 项把两类店铺的结果张冠李戴；D 项原文未提。"},{"n":48,"stem":"What can be inferred from the safety argument in paragraph 3?","options":{"A":"Cyclists themselves were to blame for most earlier collisions.","B":"Injuries fell because the road was redesigned, not because riders behaved better.","C":"Hospital admissions rose as more people took up cycling.","D":"Officials had promised safer roads before the lane was built."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段最后一句说骑车人长期被告知要更小心，而隔离之后碰撞减半、重伤入院同样下降，使他们觉得问题出在道路本身，即安全改善来自改造道路而非要求骑手自律。A 项与「长期被要求小心骑行」的语境相反；C 项把下降说成上升。"},{"n":49,"stem":"In paragraph 3, the phrase 'the same pattern' refers to the drop in","options":{"A":"the number of cyclists using the corridor.","B":"serious injuries requiring hospital treatment.","C":"the number of customers at street cafes.","D":"traffic on the parallel streets."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段第 3 句先说 collisions ... fell by half，紧接着说 hospital admissions for serious injuries followed the same pattern，可见所指就是「严重受伤入院人数同样下降」。A 项与事实相反（骑车人数是上升的）；D 项出现在第 2 段，且方向是增加。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards the cycle lane?","options":{"A":"Unreserved support for the council's decision.","B":"A cautious, balanced assessment of its effects.","C":"Firm opposition on grounds of cost.","D":"Complete indifference to the outcome."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。作者既列出骑车人数上升、事故减半等收益，也在第 4 段呈现批评者的质疑和议会自己承认的 unevenly distributed，末句更说效果 remains genuinely uncertain，可见态度谨慎而平衡。A 项「无条件支持」和 C 项「坚决反对」都只取一面；D 项与全文细致的分析不符。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"Students often treat sleep as a resource to be spent, like money, and the week before examinations is when they spend most freely. A survey of 1,200 undergraduates at three universities found that average sleep fell from seven and a half hours to five hours and forty minutes during that period, while coffee consumption doubled. The students who slept least did not, on average, do better. They did worse, and the gap was widest on questions that asked them to apply a rule to a new example rather than simply recall a fact.\nThe reason lies in what happens after learning. During deep sleep the brain replays patterns of activity recorded during the day, strengthening the connections that hold new information. This process, called consolidation, is not a luxury that can be postponed; it appears to happen mainly at night and cannot be replaced by rest during the day. In one experiment, students who learned a list of word pairs in the evening and then slept for eight hours remembered about a fifth more the next morning than those who stayed awake for the same period.\nSleep loss also damages attention, and attention is the door through which information enters memory in the first place. A tired student reading a page may reach the bottom with no memory of the middle, not because the material was hard but because the mind drifted. Laboratory studies show that after a night of restricted sleep the number of brief lapses in attention rises sharply, and that people are poor at noticing when they have occurred. Students who believe they are studying for six hours may in fact be studying effectively for three.\nThere is a further cost that is easy to overlook. Sleep affects mood and the ability to tolerate frustration, so a tired student is more likely to abandon a difficult problem and less likely to ask for help. Over a term this can turn a single bad night into a habit of avoidance. Counsellors at one university reported that students seeking help with anxiety often described a cycle in which late-night study produced poor results, which produced more anxiety, which produced later nights still.\nThe practical advice is unglamorous. Regular bedtimes matter more than total hours in bed, because the body's internal clock responds to consistency. Screens should be put away half an hour before sleep, and coffee should stop in the early afternoon. Most important, revision should be spread across days rather than packed into the final night, since each period of sleep can only strengthen what has already been learned. The student who sleeps before an examination is not being lazy; they are completing the work.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"What did the survey find about students during the examination period?","options":{"A":"They slept less and drank more coffee, and the least sleepers did worse.","B":"They slept longer but performed worse than in normal weeks.","C":"Their sleep fell, yet their results improved on recall questions.","D":"Coffee helped them keep their normal amount of sleep."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 1 段第 2 句说睡眠从七个半小时降到五小时四十分钟、咖啡消费翻倍，第 3、4 句又说睡得最少的人成绩更差，A 项三点齐全。B 项「睡得更多」与原文相反；C 项说回忆题成绩提高，而原文是「应用题上差距最大」，并非提高。"},{"n":52,"stem":"According to paragraph 2, consolidation is a process that","options":{"A":"can be replaced by resting during the daytime.","B":"happens mainly at night and strengthens new connections.","C":"takes place only while students are awake.","D":"students can practise deliberately before an exam."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 2、3 句说深度睡眠时大脑重放白天的活动模式、强化承载新信息的连接，并说这一过程主要发生在夜间，B 项对应这两点。A 项与 cannot be replaced 相反；C 项把夜间过程说成清醒时发生；D 项把无意识的生理过程说成可刻意练习的技巧。"},{"n":53,"stem":"Why does the author mention a student who reads a page but cannot recall its middle?","options":{"A":"To recommend reading difficult material a second time.","B":"To show how tiredness keeps information from entering memory.","C":"To prove that reading speed declines as people grow older.","D":"To suggest that textbooks are badly organised."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段第 1 句指出注意力是信息进入记忆的门，第 2 句随即用「读到底却记不住中间」的例子说明疲劳时 mind drifted，例子正是服务于这一观点。A 项谈重复阅读、D 项谈教材编排，都不是举例目的；C 项与年龄无关。"},{"n":54,"stem":"The word 'unglamorous' in paragraph 5 most probably means","options":{"A":"hard to follow in practice.","B":"plain and not at all exciting.","C":"expensive for most students.","D":"familiar to almost everyone."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 5 段第 1 句之后列举的都是按时睡觉、睡前放下屏幕、下午早停咖啡、把复习分散到多天等朴素建议，可见该词指「平淡、不吸引人」。A 项「难以照做」是过度推断，原文强调的恰恰是容易执行；C 项「昂贵」与建议内容毫无关系。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the main point of the passage?","options":{"A":"Students should drink less coffee before examinations.","B":"Universities should schedule examinations later in the day.","C":"Sleep is part of learning rather than time taken away from it.","D":"Memory depends mainly on how long a student reads."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。全文从记忆巩固、注意力、情绪三个层面说明睡眠参与学习，末句更直接说考前睡觉的人不是偷懒，而是在「完成工作」，C 项概括了这一主旨。A 项只是第 5 段的一个细节；B 项考试时间安排在文中未提；D 项与「以为学了六小时，实际只学了三小时」的说法相反。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on whether undergraduates should take part-time jobs. You should state your opinion and give reasons. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","prompt_cn":"校报正在征集稿件，请就「大学生是否应该做兼职」发表你的看法，写一篇 120–180 词的短文。","word_limit":120,"sample_essay":"Whether undergraduates should take part-time jobs divides students, but I believe the answer depends on how the job is chosen rather than on whether one is taken at all.\nA well-chosen job teaches what a classroom cannot. Serving customers, meeting a deadline for a small employer, or explaining a mistake to a manager builds patience and confidence that no lecture provides. It also brings a modest income, which matters to students who dislike asking their parents for money.\nYet a job becomes harmful when it consumes the hours that study needs. Students who work twenty hours a week before examinations often discover, too late, that they have traded a term of learning for a little cash. The sensible rule is to keep work under ten hours a week, to choose something related to one's field, and to stop when grades begin to fall.\nPart-time work is therefore neither a virtue nor a danger. It is a tool, and its value depends on the hand that uses it.","sample_essay_cn":"大学生是否应该做兼职，同学们看法不一，但我认为关键在于如何选择这份工作，而不在于要不要做。\n选得合适的工作能教给学生课堂教不了的东西。接待顾客、为小雇主赶上交期、向经理说明自己的失误，这些都会培养出讲座给不了的耐心与自信。它还能带来一份不多的收入，这对不愿向父母开口要钱的学生很重要。\n然而，当兼职占用了学习所需要的时间，它就有害了。考前每周工作二十小时的学生往往发现得太晚：他们用一学期的学习换来了一点现金。明智的做法是每周工作不超过十小时，尽量选择与本专业相关的岗位，并在成绩下滑时及时停下。\n因此，兼职既不是美德，也不是危险。它是一件工具，价值取决于使用它的那双手。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"中国是茶的故乡，饮茶的历史已有数千年。最初，茶叶被当作药材，后来才逐渐成为日常饮品。唐代以后，饮茶之风盛行，人们开始讲究水质、器具与冲泡方法。如今，茶不只是一种饮料，也是一种待客的方式。客人来访，主人往往先奉上一杯热茶，以示尊重。在南方的一些城市，茶馆仍是人们聊天、听书和消磨时光的地方。近年来，越来越多的年轻人重新爱上传统茶饮，这一古老习俗因此获得新的生命。","reference_en":"China is the home of tea, and the history of tea drinking there goes back thousands of years. At first tea leaves were used as a medicine, and only later did they become an everyday drink. After the Tang Dynasty the habit of tea drinking spread widely, and people began to pay attention to the quality of water, to their utensils and to the way the leaves were brewed. Today tea is not only a beverage but also a way of receiving guests. When visitors arrive, the host usually offers a cup of hot tea first as a sign of respect. In some southern cities, tea houses are still places where people chat, listen to storytellers and pass the time. In recent years more and more young people have fallen in love with traditional tea again, and this old custom has thus gained a new life.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet4-mock-2":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":2,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第2套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"Every June, something strange happens on campus. Students who arrived four years ago with two suitcases now own desks, lamps, kettles and piles of books, and most of it cannot [[26]] home with them. The campus flea market has become the answer. For one Saturday the square outside the library turns into a small town of blankets, cardboard signs and good-humoured bargaining.\n\nThe [[27]] is easy to see. A desk lamp bought for eighty yuan sells for fifteen, and a textbook that cost sixty changes hands for ten. Buyers save money, and sellers recover part of what they [[28]] in the first place. Nothing is thrown away, so the amount of waste leaving the campus [[29]] sharply each summer.\n\nOnline resale has made the same idea far more [[30]]. A student can photograph a bicycle at noon and hand it to a stranger by evening. Second-hand trading groups now have thousands of [[31]], and new posts appear at almost every hour.\n\nNot everything runs smoothly. A few sellers hide damage, and a few buyers [[32]] about a scratch that was never mentioned in the post. Many groups now ask members to provide proof of [[33]] before a post is approved, and to describe faults honestly. Trust, therefore, has to be built [[34]], one small honest deal at a time.\n\nThe deeper change is in attitude. Owning something new is no longer the only [[35]] of success. Students increasingly treat used goods as normal rather than shameful, and that shift may matter more than the money involved.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"appeal"},{"k":"B","t":"convenient"},{"k":"C","t":"charge"},{"k":"D","t":"complain"},{"k":"E","t":"falls"},{"k":"F","t":"gradually"},{"k":"G","t":"identity"},{"k":"H","t":"measure"},{"k":"I","t":"members"},{"k":"J","t":"ordinary"},{"k":"K","t":"paid"},{"k":"L","t":"private"},{"k":"M","t":"rarely"},{"k":"N","t":"travel"},{"k":"O","t":"urgent"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"N","explanation":"答案 N) travel。第 1 段第 2 句，主语 most of it 指家具和书，cannot 后须接动词原形，travel home with them 表示「随人一起运回家」；C) charge 表「要价」，不能与 home 构成此义，K) paid 是过去式，放在 cannot 后不合语法。"},{"n":27,"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A) appeal。第 2 段首句 The ___ is easy to see 需要名词作主语；下文用台灯、课本的差价说明跳蚤市场「吸引力」何在，appeal 正好；H) measure 要与 of success 搭配才成立，此处讲不通。"},{"n":28,"answer":"K","explanation":"答案 K) paid。第 2 段第 3 句 what they ___ in the first place，sellers 是当初掏钱的一方，recover part of what they paid 意为「收回当初花掉的一部分」；C) charge 指卖方要价，与 recover 的方向相反。"},{"n":29,"answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E) falls。第 2 段末句主语 the amount of waste 为单数，each summer 表明一般现在时，故用第三人称单数 falls；D) complain 的主语须是人，I) members 是名词，都不能充当谓语。"},{"n":30,"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B) convenient。第 3 段首句 far more 后需形容词，下文说中午拍照、傍晚交货，强调「方便」；J) ordinary 与 far more 连用后语义与上下文相反，O) urgent 与这种从容的交易节奏不符。"},{"n":31,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) members。第 3 段第 3 句 thousands of 后接可数名词复数，后文 new posts 也说明群里聚集的是「成员」；G) identity 是抽象不可数用法，与 thousands of 不搭配。"},{"n":32,"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D) complain。第 4 段第 2 句主语 a few buyers 为复数，about 后接抱怨的内容，complain about 是固定搭配；E) falls 为第三人称单数，与复数主语不一致。"},{"n":33,"answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G) identity。第 4 段第 3 句 provide proof of ___，proof of identity 意为「身份证明」，与 before a post is approved 的审核语境吻合；H) measure 与 proof of 无法构成搭配。"},{"n":34,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) gradually。第 4 段末句 has to be built ___，后置的 one small honest deal at a time 说明信任是「逐步」积累的，需副词；M) rarely 与「一笔笔小交易」的持续过程自相矛盾。"},{"n":35,"answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H) measure。第 5 段第 2 句 the only ___ of success，measure of success 意为「衡量成功的标准」，与 no longer 的转折呼应；C) charge 是动词，不能作 of success 的中心名词。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Ask a first-year student what volunteering means and the answer is usually narrow: helping at a charity shop, perhaps, or collecting money in the street. The reality on most campuses is much broader. Students run homework clubs, monitor air quality, guide visitors at museums, coach children's teams and sit with patients in hospital wards. Some roles last a whole term, others a single afternoon. What they share is that nobody is paid for the time. Because the options are so varied, the hardest part for a newcomer is often not commitment but choosing where to begin."},{"label":"B","text":"The largest single category is academic support. Every weekday afternoon, groups of students walk to primary schools in the neighbourhood and sit with children who need help with reading or arithmetic. The work is repetitive. A volunteer may spend an hour on the same three multiplication facts. Yet teachers say that the steady presence of the same faces matters more than any single lesson, because children who expect someone to arrive tend to arrive themselves. Volunteers are asked to commit to a full term, and those who disappear after a fortnight often do more harm than good."},{"label":"C","text":"Environmental projects attract students who prefer to see a result at the end of the day. On Saturday mornings, teams work along the river that runs behind the science buildings, pulling out bottles and plastic bags. Others audit the rubbish produced by each canteen and publish the weights on a board, which has cut food waste by a surprising amount. Planting is popular in spring, though the trees need watering for years afterwards, so the groups that survive are the ones that plan beyond the photographs. Newcomers often underestimate this second stage."},{"label":"D","text":"Hospital work is the least visible and the most demanding. Before a student is allowed onto a ward, they spend two evenings learning how to move a patient safely, what to say when someone is frightened, and when to call a nurse. The task itself looks simple: reading aloud, playing cards, listening. What makes it difficult is that patients do not always get better. Supervisors warn newcomers to expect this, and to talk it over with the group afterwards rather than carry it alone. The training is repeated each term, because students graduate and new ones arrive."},{"label":"E","text":"Large public events offer a different kind of service. When the city holds its autumn marathon, several hundred students spend a long day checking numbers, handing out water and directing runners who have lost their way. The shifts start before dawn and the streets are cold, but the atmosphere keeps people coming back. Students with a second language are placed at information points, where runners from abroad ask about transport and medical tents. Organisers value reliability above enthusiasm: a volunteer who arrives late leaves a gap that nobody can fill, and they keep a list of those who came back the following year."},{"label":"F","text":"Employers increasingly ask about volunteering, and students often mention it in interviews. The skills involved are real: organising a rota for thirty people, persuading a shop to donate fruit, explaining a complicated form to someone who cannot read it well. Yet surveys of participants suggest that very few began with a CV in mind. The benefits arrive as a by-product. Those who join mainly to decorate an application tend to leave within a month, while those who stay speak about the people they met rather than the skills they gained."},{"label":"G","text":"How do students start? Rarely through a careers fair. Most say a friend took them along once, or a teacher mentioned a project in class, or they were simply curious about a building they passed every day. The first visit is usually awkward. What keeps them is smaller and more specific: a child who finally reads a whole page, a patient who remembers their name, a runner who says thank you in broken Chinese. Motivation that begins as an accident often proves more durable than motivation that begins as a duty."},{"label":"H","text":"The commonest reason students give up is time. A single Saturday sounds generous until three essays fall due in the same week. Experienced coordinators therefore prefer short, fixed shifts to occasional heroic ones: two hours every Tuesday is easier to protect than a whole day once a month, and it is easier for the people being helped to rely on. Volunteers are also taught to say no in advance rather than disappear, because a cancelled session costs the organiser far more than a refused one."},{"label":"I","text":"Universities like to reward service with certificates, credits and annual awards. These can be useful, especially where a scholarship requires evidence of activity. But coordinators notice a pattern: when a prize is announced, applications rise and then fall away once the prize has been given. Students who stay describe a different reward, harder to put on paper, such as the moment a shy child volunteers an answer in class. The general advice is to treat recognition as a receipt, not as a reason."},{"label":"J","text":"What happens afterwards is rarely measured. A follow-up survey of graduates found that a third were still involved in some form of service ten years later, often in the same field they had entered by accident as students. Two described changing their degrees after a term on a hospital ward. The wider effect is quieter: neighbourhoods that have hosted students for years tend to trust the university more, and that trust is difficult to build by any other means. Employers in those neighbourhoods now expect students to turn up, which was not true a generation ago."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"Some roles require special preparation before a student is allowed to meet the people they serve.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。该段第 2 句说学生进入病房前要先花两个晚上学习如何安全移动病人、如何应对恐惧，正是「服务前须专门培训」；F 段谈的是服务之后的技能收获，时间顺序相反。"},{"n":37,"stem":"Certain projects produce results that can be counted, such as the weight of rubbish removed.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。该段第 3 句提到把各食堂产生的垃圾称重并把数字公布在板上，食物浪费因此下降，对应「可量化的成果」；A 段只泛谈项目种类多，没有给出任何数据。"},{"n":38,"stem":"Events sometimes need students who can communicate with participants from other countries.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。该段第 3 句说掌握第二语言的学生被安排在咨询点，为外国跑者解答交通和医疗点的问题，对应「与其他国家的参与者沟通」；B 段的服务对象是本地小学生。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Fitting service around coursework works better with short, fixed slots than with occasional long days.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。该段第 2 句直接作比较：每周二两小时比每月一整天更容易守住，也更容易让受助方依赖，即「固定短时段优于偶尔长时段」；G 段讲的是如何开始，不是如何安排时间。"},{"n":40,"stem":"The abilities developed through service are valued by employers, though they are rarely the reason students begin.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。该段第 1、2 句承认雇主看重这些能力，第 3 句随即说很少有人是抱着简历的目的开始的，与题干「被雇主看重但很少是起点」完全对应；I 段讲的是学校颁发的证书和奖项，不是雇主。"},{"n":41,"stem":"Rewards offered by institutions can weaken the spirit of the work if they become the goal.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。该段第 3 句说奖项一公布申请量就上升、发完奖又回落，末句建议把认可当成「收据而不是理由」，即外部奖励会侵蚀初衷；F 段提到的 CV 是学生自己的动机，不是机构给的奖励。"},{"n":42,"stem":"The most useful help is often steady and unexciting rather than dramatic.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。该段第 3、4 句说工作重复，一小时可能只讲三个乘法口诀，但老师认为「固定的面孔比任何一节课都重要」，对应「平淡而持续的帮助最有用」；C 段强调的是当天就能看到成果，方向相反。"},{"n":43,"stem":"Many students are drawn in by a friend or a single conversation rather than by a plan.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。该段第 2 句列举朋友带去一次、老师在课上提到、单纯出于好奇，末句说「始于偶然的动机往往更持久」，对应「被朋友或一次谈话带入而非计划」；F 段说的是留在原地的原因，不是加入的原因。"},{"n":44,"stem":"The effects of service can outlast university and reshape career choices.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。该段第 2、3 句说十年后仍有三分之一在做某种服务，还有人因此改了专业，对应「影响延续到毕业之后并改变职业选择」；F 段讲的是求职时的加分，时间范围限于在校期间。"},{"n":45,"stem":"The range of available roles is wider than most newcomers expect.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。该段第 2、3 句列举作业辅导、空气监测、博物馆引导、医院陪护等，并说新生对志愿服务的理解通常很窄，对应「可选角色比新手以为的多」；E 段只讲赛事服务这一种形式。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"It is nine in the evening and the essay is due at noon tomorrow. The student has opened the document four times. Each time she has remembered that the desk needs tidying, that a friend should be answered, that a documentary about volcanoes would take only forty minutes. Psychologists who study this behaviour do not call it laziness. They define procrastination as the voluntary delay of an intended course of action, despite expecting that the delay will leave you worse off. The word voluntary matters: the student is not unable to write, she is choosing not to begin.\n\nThe reasons are better documented than most students expect. The first is what researchers call task aversiveness: the work feels boring, difficult or embarrassing, and the mind moves away from it as a hand moves away from a hot pan. The second is fear. When a piece of work is likely to be judged, not starting protects a private belief that one could do it well if one really tried. The third is a simple error of arithmetic. People consistently underestimate how long tasks take, so a plan that looks reasonable on Monday is already impossible by Thursday. The fourth is the environment itself, where a phone offers relief within two seconds.\n\nWhat makes the habit so stubborn is the reward that arrives immediately. Putting the essay aside produces real relief, and relief is a powerful teacher. The brain learns that escape works, and the next evening the escape comes faster. The cost is paid later, in the form of guilt, rushed work and a private conviction that one is unreliable. Because the punishment is delayed, it teaches almost nothing.\n\nTreatment, fortunately, is practical rather than moral. Counsellors rarely begin by asking students to want the task. They ask them to make the first step absurdly small: open the file, write one sentence, set a timer for ten minutes. They also recommend deciding in advance, in a sentence such as 'when I sit down after breakfast, I will read the first page', because a decision made in a calm moment survives better than one made at the moment of temptation. Removing the phone from the room works better than resolving to ignore it.\n\nNone of this turns a difficult evening into an easy one. But it does suggest that the problem is not a defect of character. Procrastination is a way of managing a bad mood, and like any habit it can be replaced by a better one. Students who succeed are not those who never feel the pull of the volcano documentary; they are those who have arranged their day so that beginning takes less courage than avoiding.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"According to paragraph 1, what distinguishes procrastination from simple laziness?","options":{"A":"It involves choosing to delay even when the delay is harmful.","B":"It appears mainly when a deadline is very close.","C":"It is caused by an inability to concentrate on work.","D":"It affects students more seriously than other groups."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 1 段第 4、5 句把拖延定义为「自愿推迟既定行动，且明知推迟会让自己更糟」，并强调 voluntary 一词，可见关键在于「自己选择」；C 项说无法集中注意力，与末句 she is not unable to write 直接冲突。"},{"n":47,"stem":"What does the author mean by 'a simple error of arithmetic' in paragraph 2?","options":{"A":"Students cannot work out their grades correctly.","B":"Students underestimate how much time a task will need.","C":"Students spend too long on the easiest part of a task.","D":"Students plan their week without looking at a calendar."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 4 句把第三种原因称作 simple error of arithmetic，紧接着解释人们总是低估任务所需的时间，故 B 项；A 项谈的是算成绩，原文说的是算时间，属偷换对象。"},{"n":48,"stem":"In paragraph 2, the word 'aversiveness' most probably means ______.","options":{"A":"the quality of being unpleasant","B":"the state of being urgent","C":"the lack of clear instructions","D":"the fear of being judged"},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 2 段第 2 句用 boring, difficult or embarrassing 解释 task aversiveness，并以手躲开热锅作比，可见指「令人不适」；D 项是紧随其后的第二种原因 fear，属张冠李戴。"},{"n":49,"stem":"Why does the author say that the punishment 'teaches almost nothing'?","options":{"A":"Because students already know what the consequences will be.","B":"Because guilt is a weaker emotion than relief.","C":"Because it comes too late to be linked to the choice that caused it.","D":"Because teachers seldom punish work that is handed in late."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 3 段末两句说代价（内疚、赶工）要等到以后才付，因为惩罚被延迟，所以几乎起不到教训作用，C 项正是此意；B 项把两种情绪比强弱，原文并未作此比较。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What is the author's main point about procrastination?","options":{"A":"It reflects a fixed weakness in the student's character.","B":"It should be dealt with mainly by raising motivation.","C":"It is a way of handling mood that practice can replace.","D":"It disappears once a student organises deadlines properly."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 5 段第 2、3 句说问题不是性格缺陷，拖延是「管理坏情绪的方式」，可以像习惯一样被替换，故 C 项；B 项与第 4 段「咨询师并不先要求学生喜欢这项任务」相反。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"From the street it looks like any other office block: twelve floors of glass, a car park, a row of tired trees. Twelve floors up, the roof has been covered with shallow wooden boxes, and the boxes are full of tomatoes, beans and lavender. The garden is tended by employees on their lunch breaks and by a retired teacher from the block behind. Rooftop farming is spreading where ground is expensive, and researchers have begun to measure what it does.\n\nThe environmental case is stronger than the romantic photographs suggest. A conventional roof sends rainwater into the drains as quickly as possible; in a heavy storm, thousands of such roofs can overwhelm the system. Soil and plants hold part of that water and release it slowly, so a green roof behaves like a small, patient sponge. The plants also shade the surface beneath them and release water vapour, which can lower the temperature of the roof by several degrees and reduce the demand for air conditioning in the rooms below. The effect on the wider city is smaller but real: a cluster of green roofs can soften the urban heat island that sits over dense districts on summer nights.\n\nThe benefits that residents notice first are not ecological. Office workers who spend an hour a week among the boxes return to their screens calmer, and studies of attention suggest why: natural scenes let the mind rest in a way a corridor does not. For older residents the garden is a reason to leave the flat and a place to be useful. One woman who grew up on a farm now teaches neighbours when to pick the beans. People who had shared a wall for a decade learned each other's names over a watering can.\n\nNone of this is free. A roof must be strong enough to carry wet soil, which weighs far more than gravel, and the waterproof layer must be perfect, or leaks appear three floors down. Water must be carried up or pumped, and someone must be responsible in August when the office empties. Cost is the reason such gardens cluster in wealthier districts and in buildings with a facilities manager. A rooftop garden is also not a park: it serves one building, and it cannot replace the open ground a neighbourhood needs.\n\nThe honest conclusion is modest. A few hundred square metres of soil will not cool a city or feed it. But the gains are real, measurable and local, and they take no land that could be used for housing. Cities that want them should treat them as infrastructure rather than decoration: offer advice on weight and waterproofing, and keep the paperwork simple.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"What is the main environmental function of the soil and plants described in paragraph 2?","options":{"A":"They filter pollution from the air inside the building.","B":"They slow the flow of rainwater into the drainage system.","C":"They increase the amount of water available to the city.","D":"They protect the roof from damage caused by strong winds."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 2、3 句说普通屋顶的设计是尽快把雨水排入下水道，暴雨时会压垮系统，而土壤和植物「像一块有耐心的海绵」把水留住再慢慢释放，故 B 项；C 项说增加城市可用水量，原文强调的是减缓排放。"},{"n":52,"stem":"According to paragraph 2, how do green roofs reduce the need for air conditioning?","options":{"A":"By blocking sunlight from entering the windows.","B":"By producing cool air that flows into the rooms.","C":"By shading the roof and releasing water vapour.","D":"By reducing the number of people using the building."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 2 段第 4 句给出两条途径：plants shade the surface beneath them 与 release water vapour，从而降低屋顶温度、减少楼下房间的空调需求；A 项说遮挡窗户，把遮阴对象偷换成了窗户。"},{"n":53,"stem":"What can be inferred about the social effects of the garden in paragraph 3?","options":{"A":"It mainly benefits people who already knew one another.","B":"It brings together people who were previously strangers.","C":"It matters more to older residents than to office workers.","D":"It has taken the place of other forms of neighbourly contact."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段末两句说共用一堵墙十年的邻居在浇水壶旁才互通姓名，农场长大的老太太教年轻人何时摘豆，可见花园让原本陌生的人产生交往；A 项与「此前互不相识」正好相反。"},{"n":54,"stem":"The word 'modest' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.","options":{"A":"limited in scale","B":"difficult to prove","C":"likely to fail","D":"expensive to maintain"},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 5 段首句 The honest conclusion is modest，紧接着说几百平方米的土既不能让城市降温也养不活城市，可见 modest 指「规模有限、期望不宜过高」；D 项把 modest 误解为花钱多，与后文 gains are real 的正面评价不符。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards rooftop gardens?","options":{"A":"They offer a promising answer to the housing shortage.","B":"They are decorative projects with little practical value.","C":"They bring genuine local benefits but need official support.","D":"They should replace parks in densely built districts."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 5 段承认收益 real, measurable and local，又主张城市应把它们当作基础设施、在承重与防水上给建议并简化手续，故 C 项；D 项与第 4 段末句 cannot replace the open ground 直接矛盾。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the advantages and disadvantages of using mobile phones in class. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","prompt_cn":"以「课堂上使用手机的利与弊」为题写一篇短文，先谈好处，再谈弊端，最后给出自己的看法，字数 120–180。","word_limit":120,"sample_essay":"Mobile phones are now part of student life, and their presence in class divides teachers. The advantages are easy to see. A phone offers an instant dictionary, a calculator and a photograph of the board, and it lets a shy student check a fact without interrupting the lesson. A quick search can turn a confusing term into a clear one.\n\nThe disadvantages are harder to ignore. A single notification pulls attention away for far longer than the few seconds it takes to read, so a student who replies to a message misses the explanation that follows. Games are more attractive than grammar, and the student at the back may be lost for the whole hour.\n\nIn my view, the answer is not a complete ban. Teachers can set clear moments when phones are allowed, such as a short research task, and ask for them to be face down at other times. Rules that students understand are followed more often than rules that are simply announced.","sample_essay_cn":"手机如今已是学生生活的一部分，它在课堂上的存在让老师们意见不一。好处显而易见：手机能当即时词典、计算器，也能拍下板书；害羞的学生不必打断讲课就能核实一个事实，一次快速搜索还能把含糊的术语变得清楚。\n\n弊端则更难忽视。一条通知夺走注意力的时间，远长于读它的那几秒钟，回消息的学生往往错过紧接着的讲解。游戏比语法更有吸引力，坐在后排的学生可能整节课都在走神。\n\n在我看来，答案并不是一律禁止。老师可以划定明确的时段允许使用手机，例如做一次简短的资料查找，其余时间则要求屏幕朝下放好。学生理解的规则，比单纯宣布的规则更容易被遵守。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"中国高铁的发展速度在世界上少见。二十多年前，从北京到上海坐火车要十几个小时；如今同样路程只需四个多小时。高铁把许多中小城市连接起来，年轻人能在大城市工作，周末回家看望父母。车厢安静整洁，乘客用手机就能买票、查到站时间。高铁不只改变出行方式，也带动了沿线城市的旅游和商业。一些偏远县城通了高铁，开起民宿和餐馆，当地人收入明显增加。也有人觉得票价偏高，但多数旅客认为省下的时间更宝贵。","reference_en":"Few countries have developed high-speed rail as quickly as China. More than twenty years ago, a train journey from Beijing to Shanghai took over ten hours; today the same distance takes a little more than four. The network links many small and medium-sized cities, so young people can keep a job in a big city and still go home at weekends to see their parents. The carriages are quiet and clean, and passengers can buy tickets and check arrival times on their phones. High-speed rail has changed the way people travel, and it has also brought tourism and business to the cities along the lines. Some counties that were once remote now have guesthouses and restaurants, and local incomes have risen noticeably. A few travellers feel that tickets are expensive, but for most of them the hours saved are just as valuable.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet4-mock-3":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":3,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第3套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"Few households set out to waste food. Trouble usually begins at the supermarket, where a hungry shopper fills the trolley with more than the fridge can hold. A survey of 400 families found that most kitchens [[26]] nearly a fifth of the food they buy, and that the money lost this way is rarely noticed.\n\nThe first remedy is planning. Before shopping, families should open the fridge and write a short list. Shoppers who [[27]] closely to that list buy far fewer items on impulse, and a [[28]] basket filled twice a week keeps vegetables fresher than one huge weekly load.\n\nStorage matters just as much. Many people [[29]] the same mistake: they put everything in one drawer, where soft fruit and greens spoil faster. Bread keeps longer in the freezer than on the counter, and herbs stay fresh in a glass of water. Containers marked with the date make the oldest item easy to [[30]], so it is eaten first.\n\nLeftovers deserve a second chance. Cold rice becomes fried rice; roast vegetables turn into soup. Families that set aside one evening a week for leftovers report a clear [[31]]: they bin far less and spend less at the till. Children pick up the [[32]] quickly, and a half loaf becomes tomorrow's lunch.\n\nNone of this demands special equipment or extra money. It asks only for a [[33]] approach to buying food and storing it, and for enough patience to [[34]] yesterday's dinner into today's lunch. Households that adopt these routines often find their weekly [[35]] falls by a quarter.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"benefit"},{"k":"B","t":"bill"},{"k":"C","t":"carefully"},{"k":"D","t":"devote"},{"k":"E","t":"generous"},{"k":"F","t":"habit"},{"k":"G","t":"identify"},{"k":"H","t":"modest"},{"k":"I","t":"rarely"},{"k":"J","t":"repeat"},{"k":"K","t":"sensible"},{"k":"L","t":"shortage"},{"k":"M","t":"stick"},{"k":"N","t":"turn"},{"k":"O","t":"discard"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"O","explanation":"答案 O) discard。第 1 段第 3 句，most kitchens 是复数主语，空格处需及物动词原形，宾语为 nearly a fifth of the food，discard 意为「丢弃」，与后半句 the money lost 呼应；N) turn 要与 into 搭配才表「变成」，此处没有 into；D) devote 须接 to，语法也不成立。"},{"n":27,"answer":"M","explanation":"答案 M) stick。第 2 段第 3 句，who 引导定语从句修饰 Shoppers，stick to 意为「坚持照……做」，与后半句 buy far less on impulse 构成因果；J) repeat 不能接 to，D) devote 虽可接 to，但 devote to that list 缺宾语，语义不通。"},{"n":28,"answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H) modest。同句 a ___ basket 需形容词，后面 than one huge load 构成对比，说明购物篮是「少量的、适度的」；E) generous 意为「大量的」，与 huge 同义，会使这个比较失去意义。"},{"n":29,"answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J) repeat。第 3 段第 2 句，Many people 后接动词原形，repeat the same mistake 是固定搭配「重犯同一个错误」；O) discard 与 mistake 搭配不成立，M) stick 后面必须接 to。"},{"n":30,"answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G) identify。第 3 段末句，easy to 后接动词原形，贴上日期是为了「认出」最旧的那一件，与 so it is eaten first 逻辑一致；N) turn 需 into 才成立，J) repeat 与 the oldest item 搭配不通。"},{"n":31,"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A) benefit。第 4 段第 3 句，a clear ___ 后接冒号，冒号后的 bin far less and spend less 正是好处，故填 benefit；L) shortage 表示「短缺」，与「扔得更少」正好相反。"},{"n":32,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) habit。第 4 段末句，pick up the ___ 是固定搭配「养成……习惯」，后文孩子把半个面包留作午饭正是这一习惯的体现；A) benefit 已用于上一空，且 pick up the benefit 不成立。"},{"n":33,"answer":"K","explanation":"答案 K) sensible。第 5 段第 1 句，a ___ approach 需形容词修饰 approach，only 引出与 no special equipment 相对的「只需明智的做法」；E) generous 修饰 approach 语义空泛，也与全文省钱的主旨相反。"},{"n":34,"answer":"N","explanation":"答案 N) turn。第 5 段第 1 句，patience to 后接动词原形，turn A into B 意为「把 A 变成 B」，指把剩饭变成第二天的午饭；O) discard 不与 into 搭配，M) stick 后面须接 to。"},{"n":35,"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B) bill。第 5 段第 2 句，their weekly ___ falls by a quarter，空格被 weekly 修饰且与 falls 搭配，指每周的账单或开销下降，与全文省钱主题一致；A) benefit 不能与 falls 连用表示减少，L) shortage 与 falls 语义冲突。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Walk down Halden Road on any weekday morning and you will pass a strip of grass no wider than a bus. Ten years ago it held a bench, a litter bin and very little else. Today the same strip holds twelve pieces of outdoor exercise equipment, a drinking fountain and a rubberised surface that stays firm in the rain. The district council installed the machines in 2019 and has never charged anyone to use them. Residents call the place the pocket park, and on a fine morning you may have to wait your turn at the air walker."},{"label":"B","text":"The machines are not the heavy steel frames found in commercial gyms. They use the user's own body weight as resistance, so a cross trainer slows down when the person pushing it slows down, and nobody has to set a speed. Most are low impact: the feet never leave the pedals, the handles sit within easy reach of someone who prefers to remain seated, and no exercise needs a partner. A metal plate on each machine shows three simple diagrams and one short sentence in large print, because the designers assumed that most users would arrive alone."},{"label":"C","text":"The park has three busy periods, and they barely overlap. Between six and eight in the morning it belongs to people over sixty, who arrive in pairs, work through the same sequence of machines in the same order and leave before the shops open. From four in the afternoon the equipment is taken over by teenagers, who treat the pull-down bars as a climbing frame, which the council did not plan for. Office workers appear briefly around one o'clock, still wearing their lanyards, and rarely stay more than twenty minutes."},{"label":"D","text":"For the first two years the park was empty after sunset. The nearest street lamp stood forty metres away, and residents said they did not want to exercise where they could not see their own feet. In 2021 the council added four low lamps on three-metre posts, angled so that the light falls on the equipment rather than into the windows of the flats opposite. Evening use rose sharply within a month. The change was largest among women, many of whom had avoided the park altogether after dark. Two of the lamps were damaged by a car within a year, and the council replaced both within a week."},{"label":"E","text":"Every Saturday at nine, a retired PE teacher called Ms. Nora Pike runs a free session that lasts forty-five minutes. She begins with ten minutes of slow movement for stiff shoulders, then takes the group through the machines in a fixed order, and finishes with stretching on the grass. Numbers grew from six to more than thirty within a year, and the clinic on the corner now sends a nurse once a month to check blood pressure and answer questions. Nobody books a place; people simply turn up. Ms. Pike also keeps a paper list of who has been shown how to use each machine, because new faces appear every month."},{"label":"F","text":"The equipment cost about as much as a small car, and the district spends roughly two thousand a year keeping it in working order. A hardware shop on the corner covers part of that sum in exchange for a modest sign on the fence. A private gym membership in this city costs more for one person than the annual maintenance of the whole park, which is why the council's finance officer calls the site cheap insurance. He also argues that it reduces demand for other services."},{"label":"G","text":"Not everything has gone smoothly. Two machines stood out of order for five months because the parts had to be ordered from another province, and a bearing was stolen twice in the same winter. Households close to the fence have complained about the thump of cross trainers at seven in the morning and about groups who stay talking until midnight. The council now posts a repair date on the fence whenever a machine fails, a small change that has done more for goodwill than any public meeting. The stolen bearing was finally replaced with a lockable cover, which has so far held."},{"label":"H","text":"A survey of 600 local residents, carried out by a university team, found that 41 per cent of those living within five minutes' walk said they exercised more than they had before the park opened. That number should be read with care. The survey relied on what people said about themselves, and residents who never visit the park were less likely to return the form at all. Even so, the pattern held across three years of data and in every age group. Older men were the least likely to report a change, and the team could not explain why."},{"label":"I","text":"What the survey could not measure was the effect on neighbours. People who had lived on the same street for a decade met for the first time beside the air walker. A group of regulars now runs a phone chat that announces who is coming out, shares photographs of a broken handle and organises a shared taxi to the hospital for members who need one. For several older residents this is the only conversation of the day, and it began beside a machine that costs nothing to use. The chat has sixty-four members, most of whom had never joined any group before."},{"label":"J","text":"Other districts have asked how to copy the idea, and the honest answer is less attractive than the photographs. A strip of unused land, twelve machines, four lamps and one volunteer are enough to begin. What cannot be skipped is the dull part: a maintenance contract, a named person to telephone when something breaks, and a meeting with the people who live close enough to hear the equipment at seven in the morning. Without those three things, the park is scrap metal with good intentions."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"A piece of ground that once served almost no purpose now offers equipment that costs local people nothing.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。该段说这块地十年前只有长椅和垃圾桶，如今装了十二件器械，并且安装后从未向任何人收费，与「几乎没用途的地块」「免费」两点对应；F 段虽然谈到钱，但讲的是维护成本，不是使用是否收费。"},{"n":37,"stem":"The equipment responds to how hard the user works instead of setting a fixed pace for everyone.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。该段第 2 句说器械以使用者自身体重为阻力，人推得慢机器就慢，谁也不必设定速度，正是「随使用者用力大小而变化」；C 段谈的是不同时段的使用人群，与此无关。"},{"n":38,"stem":"The same space is used by different age groups at different times of the day.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。该段按早晨、下午、午后一点三个时段分别写老年人、青少年和上班族，且开头就说三个高峰「几乎不重叠」；A 段只笼统提到有人排队，没有区分人群与时段。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Making the site visible after dark produced a quick increase in use, especially among women.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。该段说装灯前日落后无人使用，2021 年加装四盏矮灯后一个月内晚间使用量骤增，且女性变化最大，正合题意；B 段讲器械的低冲击设计，与照明无关。"},{"n":40,"stem":"A weekly class is run without payment by a local person and has attracted help from a health service.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。该段说退休体育老师 Ms. Nora Pike 每周六免费带课，街角诊所每月派护士量血压，对应「不收费的本地人主持」「医疗机构参与」；G 段虽然提到居民，但讲的是投诉与维修。"},{"n":41,"stem":"The amount needed to keep the site running is tiny next to what private facilities charge.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。该段把每年约两千元的维护费与私人健身房一个人的年费相比，并称这块场地是「便宜的保险」，正是运营成本远低于商业场馆；E 段谈课程人数增长，不涉及费用比较。"},{"n":42,"stem":"Missing parts and theft have left some of the equipment out of action.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。该段说两台机器因零件要从外省订购而停用五个月，同一冬天还两次丢失轴承，对应「配件不到位」和「被盗」；D 段讲的是照明改造带来的好处，方向相反。"},{"n":43,"stem":"The survey results come with a warning about the way the information was gathered.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。该段给出 41% 这个数字后立刻说「应谨慎看待」，理由是数据来自自我报告，且不去公园的人更不可能交回问卷，正是对调查方式的提醒；I 段说的是调查无法测量的邻里关系。"},{"n":44,"stem":"The site has brought together people who had never spoken to one another before.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。该段说同一条街上住了十年的邻居第一次在漫步机旁相识，还自发建了手机群互相照应，对应「此前从未交谈的人建立了联系」；C 段只说明不同人群分时段使用，并未涉及彼此交往。"},{"n":45,"stem":"Repeating the scheme elsewhere depends more on arrangements for upkeep and for consulting residents than on the machines themselves.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。该段说土地、器械、灯和一名志愿者就够起步，真正不能省的是维修合同、报修负责人和与附近居民的沟通会，强调「难的是维护与协商，不是硬件」；F 段只讲钱，没有提到征询居民意见。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Ask people what makes a city unhealthy and most will mention dirty air, traffic or a lack of green space. Few will say noise, although noise is the pollutant we meet most often. A study of three European cities estimated that traffic sound above 65 decibels during the day reaches one household in five, and linked years of exposure to raised blood pressure and broken sleep. What makes noise odd among pollutants is that it leaves no residue: once the lorry has passed, the street looks clean again.\n\nThe body, however, does not forget so quickly. Sound is processed first as a warning rather than as information. A sudden bang reaches the brain before we have decided whether it matters, and the body answers with a small rise in heart rate and a tightening of the muscles. If the noise repeats, the response repeats, even when the sleeper does not wake. Researchers who measured sleep in rooms beside a motorway found that people who said they had grown used to the sound still showed brief rises in heart rate a hundred times a night.\n\nThe effects are not evenly spread. Households that can afford triple glazing, thick walls and a bedroom at the back of the building buy silence; those in cheaper flats beside main roads cannot. A study of schoolchildren found that reading scores were lower in classrooms where aircraft passed overhead, and that the gap widened as the school year went on. Teachers in those rooms also spoke more loudly and took more sick leave.\n\nSolutions exist, and some are surprisingly cheap. Lower speed limits cut tyre noise more than most drivers expect, and quiet road surfaces, planted strips and a moved bus stop can each remove a few decibels. None of these measures is dramatic, and none produces the visible result that a new park or a cleaned river does. That may be why noise receives so little attention: a problem you cannot photograph is easy to postpone.\n\nThe most effective step, researchers argue, is not technical but political. Cities that treat noise as a public health matter set limits, measure them and publish the results, in the way they already do for drinking water. Where that has happened, complaints have fallen and, more importantly, so has exposure. The lesson is simple enough: a city that listens to its residents should also listen to its noise.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What does the author find most distinctive about noise as a pollutant?","options":{"A":"It disappears from view as soon as its source has moved on.","B":"It is produced mainly by vehicles in the centre of cities.","C":"It can be measured more accurately than the quality of air.","D":"It harms wealthy households more severely than poorer ones."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 1 段末句说噪音不留下残留物，卡车一过街道看上去又干净了，正是「源头离开后便从视野中消失」；D 项与第 3 段相反，原文说买得起三层玻璃和背街卧室的富裕家庭可以把安静买下来。"},{"n":47,"stem":"What did the sleep studies show about people who believed they had got used to traffic noise?","options":{"A":"They woke up far more often than they realised.","B":"Their bodies still reacted to the sound many times a night.","C":"They needed longer than light sleepers to fall asleep.","D":"They reported fewer health problems than their neighbours."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段末句说自认为已经习惯噪音的人，一夜之中仍有上百次心率的短暂上升，说明身体仍在反应；A 项偷换概念，原文强调的是「没有醒来」也有反应，而非醒来次数多。"},{"n":48,"stem":"Why does the author mention triple glazing and bedrooms at the back?","options":{"A":"To explain why new buildings are quieter than old ones.","B":"To show that protection from noise depends on income.","C":"To recommend a cheap solution that councils can fund.","D":"To question whether silence is really worth paying for."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段开头用「买得起」与「住不起」对照，随后说便宜公寓里的住户做不到，可见作者要说明安静是可以花钱买到的、因而分配不均；C 项把三层玻璃当成政府可资助的低成本方案，原文并未这样说。"},{"n":49,"stem":"What does the author suggest by saying that a problem you cannot photograph is easy to postpone?","options":{"A":"Officials keep delaying action because the damage is invisible.","B":"Noise will fade away by itself as cities grow richer.","C":"The problem is too complex for scientists to measure.","D":"It receives more attention than water pollution does."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 4 段先说降噪措施都不「戏剧化」、没有新公园那样的可见成果，接着用这句话解释噪音为何少受关注，即看不见的损害使决策者一再拖延；C 项与第 5 段「设限、测量、公布」矛盾。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What is the main message of the passage?","options":{"A":"Noise harms health in hidden ways and deserves public action.","B":"Traffic noise is the main reason city residents move away.","C":"Cheap technical fixes have already solved most noise problems.","D":"Scientists still disagree about whether noise affects sleep."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。全文先讲噪音对血压和睡眠的隐性伤害，再讲其影响不均、措施不受重视，末段主张把噪音当作公共卫生问题来测量和公布，主旨即「隐性危害需要公共行动」；C 项与第 4 段「没有一项措施引人注目」以及末段「关键不在技术」都不符。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"Parents are often told that a second language is good for the brain, and the claim usually rests on studies showing that bilingual children do better on certain puzzles. The picture is more complicated than the advertisement suggests. Children who grow up with two languages do perform better on tasks that require ignoring a distraction, but the advantage is modest and does not appear in every study.\n\nThe most reliable finding concerns what researchers call executive control: the set of mental skills that let a person hold a goal in mind, switch between rules and resist an obvious but wrong answer. A bilingual speaker chooses between two words for every object and must hold back the one that is not wanted, thousands of times a day. Practice of that kind may strengthen the system that manages attention, much as repeated lifting strengthens an arm.\n\nThe effect on memory is less clear. Some studies report that people who take up a language in later life score better on attention tests a year or two later; others find no difference once education and income are taken into account. A 2022 review of forty studies concluded that the average benefit was small, and that the studies with the weakest methods reported the largest gains, a pattern that should make any reader cautious.\n\nWhat the research does support strongly is a different argument. Learning a language forces a learner to notice how meaning is built: why one word order sounds natural and another does not, why a sentence can be polite in one culture and rude in another. That habit of noticing does not require fluency. Adults who study for an hour a week for a year often report that they read their own language more carefully.\n\nThe practical advice that follows is unglamorous. Choose a language you have a reason to use, practise in short sessions rather than long ones, and speak before you feel ready. Claims that a new language will add years to your mental life are probably exaggerated; claims that it will change how you notice the world are easier to defend. Either way, the first lesson is worth more than the tenth article about the benefits.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"What does the author say about the claim that bilingual children do better on puzzles?","options":{"A":"It rests on a single study that has never been repeated.","B":"It is broadly true, but the advantage is limited and not universal.","C":"It applies only to children who start before the age of three.","D":"It was withdrawn after later researchers failed to agree."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段第 3 句承认双语儿童在需要忽略干扰的任务上确实表现更好，但随即说 the advantage is modest and does not appear in every study，即优势有限且并非普遍；D 项说该说法已被撤回，原文只说情况比广告复杂，并未否定这一发现。"},{"n":52,"stem":"Why does the author mention lifting an arm in paragraph 2?","options":{"A":"To show that language learning requires physical training.","B":"To suggest that children learn faster than adults do.","C":"To illustrate how repeated practice may strengthen attention.","D":"To argue that learners should exercise before studying."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 2 段先解释双语者每天上千次抑制不用的词，再用 much as repeated lifting strengthens an arm 作类比，说明这种反复练习可能强化管理注意力的系统；A 项把比喻当成事实，原文并非在谈体育锻炼。"},{"n":53,"stem":"What did the 2022 review of forty studies find?","options":{"A":"The average benefit was small, and weaker studies reported larger gains.","B":"Older learners gained far more than younger ones in every study.","C":"Memory improved clearly once education was taken into account.","D":"Most of the forty studies produced almost identical results."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 3 段末句说该综述认为平均收益很小，而且方法最弱的研究报告的收益最大；C 项与同段前一句相反，原文说一旦把教育和收入考虑进去，有些研究就找不到差异。"},{"n":54,"stem":"According to paragraph 4, which benefit does not depend on becoming fluent?","options":{"A":"A sharper awareness of how meaning is constructed.","B":"A larger vocabulary in the learner's first language.","C":"A better memory for names and dates.","D":"A stronger ability to imitate native pronunciation."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 4 段说学语言迫使人注意意义是如何构成的，并明确 That habit of noticing does not require fluency，与选项对应；B、C、D 三项原文均未提及，属于无据推断。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards popular claims about language learning?","options":{"A":"Cautious about the size of the benefits, but not dismissive of them.","B":"Convinced that the real benefits have been greatly understated.","C":"Indifferent, since the evidence cannot be interpreted at all.","D":"Hostile, because such claims are used to sell courses."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。末段说「延长心智寿命」之类的说法大概被夸大，但「改变你观察世界的方式」较站得住脚，可见作者对收益大小持保留态度却不全盘否定；D 项过度，原文虽提到 advertisement，却未指责其动机。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay. Suppose a foreign friend is coming to your university and you want to introduce a traditional Chinese festival to him or her. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","prompt_cn":"假设一位外国朋友要来你的学校，你想向他（她）介绍一个中国传统节日。写一篇 120–180 词的短文，说明这个节日的由来或习俗，并表达欢迎之意。","word_limit":120,"sample_essay":"Dear Daniel,\n\nSince you arrive in early February, you will be here for the Spring Festival, and I would like to tell you what to expect. It is the most important holiday in China, and it is less a single day than a season of about two weeks.\n\nBefore the holiday, families clean the house from top to bottom, paste red paper cuttings on the windows and cook far more food than anyone can eat. On the eve of the new year, everyone gathers for a long dinner; dumplings appear in the north, while rice cakes are common in the south. Older relatives hand young people red envelopes with money inside, and children stay up late to welcome the new year.\n\nIf you join my family, I will take you to the night market near my home, where lanterns hang along the street. Please do not worry about the food or the customs. Just follow us and ask whatever you like.\n\nYours,\nWei","sample_essay_cn":"亲爱的丹尼尔：\n\n你二月初到，正好赶上春节，我想先告诉你一些该期待的事。春节是中国最重要的节日，它与其说是一天，不如说是持续约两周的一段日子。\n\n节前，家家户户把房子彻底打扫一遍，在窗户上贴红色剪纸，做的饭菜多得谁也吃不完。除夕夜，全家人聚在一起吃一顿长长的晚饭；北方吃饺子，南方则常见年糕。长辈会给晚辈装着钱的红色信封，孩子们熬到很晚迎接新年。\n\n如果你来我家，我会带你去我家附近的夜市，那里沿街挂着灯笼。吃的和习俗你都不用担心，跟着我们走，想问什么就问什么。\n\n你的朋友\n伟"},"translation":{"source_cn":"中国书法是一门用毛笔书写汉字的艺术，已有两千多年历史。写字的人握住毛笔，蘸上墨，在宣纸上留下粗细、快慢各不相同的线条。同一个字，不同的人写出来往往风格完全不同，因此书法常被看作书写者性情与修养的体现。学书法一般从临摹古人的碑帖开始，先练基本笔画，再练整字的结构。许多人练字时心会慢慢静下来，所以书法也被当作一种修身养性的方式。今天，书法课已经走进中小学课堂，年轻人也愿意在周末去美术馆看展览。","reference_en":"Chinese calligraphy is the art of writing Chinese characters with a brush, and it has a history of more than two thousand years. The writer holds the brush, dips it in ink and leaves lines of different thickness and speed on the paper. The same character, written by different people, often looks completely different, so calligraphy is often seen as a reflection of the writer's character and cultivation. Learning calligraphy usually begins with copying the stone inscriptions of earlier masters: basic strokes first, then the structure of whole characters. Many people find that their minds gradually settle while they practise, which is why calligraphy is also regarded as a way of calming and improving oneself. Today calligraphy classes have entered primary and secondary schools, and young people are willing to spend weekends looking at exhibitions in art galleries.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet4-mock-4":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet4","year":2026,"month":1,"set":4,"title":"原创模拟 四级 第4套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"Every week a household throws away kilograms of peelings, coffee grounds and stale bread. In a landfill this waste rots without air and releases methane, a gas far stronger than carbon dioxide. Composting at home offers a simple [[26]].\nThe method is not difficult, and the equipment costs nothing, but it does demand a little [[27]]. Keep scraps in a lidded container and empty it every few days, before the smell becomes noticeable. Gather dry material too, such as leaves and torn cardboard, because a heap needs both wet and dry matter.\nA common mistake is adding vegetable waste only. Such a pile turns wet and [[28]], and it soon smells of vinegar rather than of earth. Aim for one part green to two parts brown, and turn the heap so that air can [[29]] the centre.\nLift the lid after a month and the scraps have gone. What happens inside is a slow form of [[30]]: bacteria break the material into a dark, crumbly substance that smells of forest soil. In warm weather the heap may [[31]] in two months; in winter it takes far longer.\nThe finished compost can be spread on flower beds, where it [[32]] moisture and feeds plants. Nothing is burned and nothing leaves the garden. The [[33]] are plain: less rubbish and better soil.\nEven in a flat, a sealed bucket on a balcony can [[34]] much of the same work, with care. The habit costs nothing, and it [[35]] a weekly chore into something close to a pleasure.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"benefits"},{"k":"B","t":"decay"},{"k":"C","t":"mature"},{"k":"D","t":"patience"},{"k":"E","t":"perform"},{"k":"F","t":"reach"},{"k":"G","t":"retains"},{"k":"H","t":"shallow"},{"k":"I","t":"solution"},{"k":"J","t":"sour"},{"k":"K","t":"steadily"},{"k":"L","t":"steady"},{"k":"M","t":"turns"},{"k":"N","t":"unless"},{"k":"O","t":"usually"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) solution。第 1 段末句说家庭堆肥 offers a simple 某物，前两句讲厨余进填埋场会释放甲烷，空格需单数名词并与 offers 搭配，solution 指「解决办法」。B) decay 指腐烂本身，不是办法；D) patience 无法与 offers a simple 搭配。"},{"n":27,"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D) patience。第 2 段第 1 句以 but 转折，前面说方法和设备都不花钱，转折后说 it does demand a little 某物，a little 后接不可数名词，patience 与「不难但要有耐性」呼应。A) benefits 是复数，不能跟在 a little 后；B) decay 在此语义不通。"},{"n":28,"answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J) sour。第 3 段第 2 句 Such a pile 指只放菜叶的堆，turns wet and 后需形容词，紧接着的 smells of vinegar 就是线索，醋是酸的。H) shallow 与 L) steady 都不能与 wet 并列描写一个腐坏的堆。"},{"n":29,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) reach。第 3 段末句 so that air can 后需动词原形，翻堆的目的是让空气进到堆心，reach the centre 即「到达中心」。E) perform 与 C) mature 都不能以 air 作主语、以 the centre 作宾语。"},{"n":30,"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B) decay。第 4 段第 2 句 a slow form of 后需名词，冒号后 bacteria break the material into a dark, crumbly substance 正是对腐烂过程的解释。D) patience 与 I) solution 都无法被冒号后的内容解释。"},{"n":31,"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C) mature。第 4 段末句主语是 the heap，may 后接动词原形，in two months 说的是堆肥「成熟」所需时间，与后半句 in winter it takes far longer 形成对照。F) reach 与 G) retains 都不能表示堆肥已经可用。"},{"n":32,"answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G) retains。第 5 段第 1 句 where 从句的主语 it 指 compost，需第三人称单数谓语，retains moisture 指保住水分，与并列的 feeds plants 一致。M) turns 要接 into 才能表示「变成」，此处不成立。"},{"n":33,"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A) benefits。第 5 段末句 the ___ are plain 中的 are 说明主语是复数名词，冒号后 less rubbish and better soil 列举的正是好处。I) solution 是单数，与 are 不一致；C) mature 是动词，不能作主语。"},{"n":34,"answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E) perform。第 6 段第 1 句 can 后需动词原形，perform much of the same work 指「完成大部分同样的活儿」，与前半句 Even in a flat 的让步呼应。M) turns 需搭配 into；G) retains 是第三人称单数形式，不能跟在 can 后。"},{"n":35,"answer":"M","explanation":"答案 M) turns。第 6 段末句主语 it 指 the habit，需第三人称单数谓语，turn A into B 是固定搭配，指把每周的杂活变成一件近乎愉快的事。E) perform 不能接 into 结构；G) retains 与 into 搭配后语义不通。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For most of its history Calderport closed at nine. The last buses left the centre, shutters came down, and the streets between the station and the river belonged to taxis and cleaners. That pattern began to change about eight years ago, when the council noticed that the city was losing young workers to a larger neighbour forty minutes away. A night-time strategy was drawn up, not by one department but by a small group that included police officers, transport planners and venue owners. Its aim was narrow and practical: keep the centre alive for four more hours, and make those hours safe, affordable and worth travelling for."},{"label":"B","text":"The Riverside night market is the strategy's most visible result. It began with twenty stalls on a disused loading yard and now runs to a hundred and eighty, open from six in the evening until one. Traders pay a modest daily fee and must hold a food hygiene certificate; the market supplies bins, water and a generator so that nobody runs a cable across the pavement. The rules are enforced by a committee of the traders themselves, which has proved cheaper and quicker than sending inspectors. For many vendors the market is a first business: one noodle stall now employs six people and rents a small kitchen nearby."},{"label":"C","text":"Bookshops were expected to disappear, yet three in Calderport now stay open all night. The oldest, on Quarry Street, was rescued by its owner, Lena Marsh, who cut back the shelves at the front and put in a coffee counter. Between eleven and two the shop sells more coffee than books, but the books are why people come: students revise at the long table, and a reading group meets on Thursdays at midnight. Ms Marsh says the late hours pay for themselves twice over, because customers who arrive at midnight often return on Saturday afternoon and buy a novel on their way out."},{"label":"D","text":"Transport was the hardest part. A city that empties at nine has no reason to run buses at midnight, and a night economy with no way home is a night economy for people who own cars. The council therefore funded a single route, N7, linking the station, the university and the riverside, with buses every twenty minutes until half past two. In its first year the service carried far more passengers than the forecast had predicted, and the cost of each journey fell as a result. Two more routes were added last spring, and a fourth is being tested at weekends."},{"label":"E","text":"Noise is where enthusiasm meets resistance. Residents near the riverside complained about shouting, about bottles being emptied at two in the morning, and about music from a courtyard bar. Rather than ban anything, the council drew a quiet zone covering four streets, set a limit on sound after eleven, and paid for double glazing in the worst-affected flats. Venues that broke the limit twice lost their late licence for a month. Complaints fell by roughly a third within a year, and, more surprisingly, the number of venues applying for late licences rose, because the rules were now predictable."},{"label":"F","text":"Safety work is less visible and harder to measure. The council replaced the lighting along three alleys, moved two taxi ranks closer to the market, and trained forty volunteers to walk people to their cars or bus stops between eleven and two. The scheme, called Safe Walk, is advertised on cards in bar toilets. A survey of women using the centre after dark found that the share who felt safe walking alone had risen from forty-one to sixty-eight per cent in two years. Reported assaults did not fall, but the police attribute that to more people being willing to report them."},{"label":"G","text":"Behind the bars and stalls is a workforce that the strategy documents rarely mention. Most staff are paid by the hour, finish after midnight and depend on the night buses to get home. A hospitality group representing two hundred workers asked the council for two things: a guaranteed ride home for anyone finishing after one, and a rule that shifts be published a week in advance. The first was agreed and is now funded from a small levy on late licences. The second is still being negotiated, and some owners argue that they cannot predict demand that far ahead."},{"label":"H","text":"Not every district can copy the riverside. An economist at the city university points out that a night economy needs density: enough people within a short walk to make a stall, a bus and a lit street worthwhile. Two outer districts tried to launch their own markets and both closed within a year, one because the nearest bus stop was a twenty-minute walk away and the other because there was nowhere for customers to park. She also warns against treating bars as the only form of night activity, since a street of bars attracts a narrower crowd than a street of bars, bookshops and food."},{"label":"I","text":"Culture has become part of the argument. The city appointed a night-time commissioner, a part-time post held by a former festival director, whose job is to speak for the hours after dark in meetings that usually end at six. Under her influence the museum opens free on the last Friday of each month until eleven, and a disused warehouse now hosts small concerts. Critics say the programme is thin: a monthly opening and a few concerts do not make a culture, and most of what the city counts as night life is simply consumption with the lights on. Attendance figures suggest the audience is growing, if slowly."},{"label":"J","text":"What would count as success? The council's own targets are economic: turnover, jobs, visitors. Others would add quieter measures, such as whether a nurse finishing at midnight can get home without paying for a taxi, or whether a resident can sleep with the window open in July. The next five years will test whether the two sets of aims can be held together, because the market, the buses and the late licences all depend on the same crowded streets, and a street can only hold so much before the people who live on it begin to leave."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"A pilot bus route drew more riders than expected, which brought down the cost of carrying each one.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。该段第 4 句说 N7 首年载客量 far more than the forecast had predicted，第 5 句说 the cost of each journey fell as a result，与题干 more riders than expected 和 brought down the cost 对应。C 段谈的是书店的营业时间，与公交无关。"},{"n":37,"stem":"Better lighting and volunteer escorts raised women's sense of safety, while recorded attacks stayed at the same level.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。该段讲更换小巷路灯、增设出租车点以及 Safe Walk 志愿陪送，接着说感到安全的女性比例由百分之四十一升到六十八，而 Reported assaults did not fall，与题干 raised ... sense of safety 和 stayed at the same level 一致。E 段讲噪音治理，不涉及安全感。"},{"n":38,"stem":"Stallholders at the market run it themselves and pay a small charge for each day of trading.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。该段第 3 句说摊主要交 a modest daily fee，第 4 句说规则由 a committee of the traders themselves 执行，与题干 run it themselves 和 small charge for each day 对应。G 段讲的是受雇员工的工资与排班，不是摊主自治。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Some critics doubt that occasional openings and concerts add up to a real cultural life.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。该段倒数第 2 句引批评者的话，说一个月一次的免费开放和几场小音乐会 do not make a culture，与题干 occasional openings and concerts 和 add up to a real cultural life 对应。J 段讨论的是如何衡量成功，不是文化含量。"},{"n":40,"stem":"The city's evenings were once empty, and the change was designed by a small group drawn from several services.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。该段开头说城市过去九点就关门，第 4 句说夜间策略由 a small group that included police officers, transport planners and venue owners 制定，与题干 a small group drawn from several services 对应。D 段只讲交通这一项，范围过窄。"},{"n":41,"stem":"Efforts to reproduce the riverside success in outer areas collapsed because of poor transport and parking.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。该段第 3 句说两个外围区试办夜市都在一年内关闭，原因分别是最近的公交站要走二十分钟和无处停车，与题干 poor transport and parking 对应。B 段讲的是河畔夜市本身如何运作，不是复制失败。"},{"n":42,"stem":"One bookshop earns more from drinks after midnight than from books, but books are what draw people in.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。该段第 3 句说十一点到两点之间咖啡卖得比书多，紧接 but the books are why people come，与题干 earns more from drinks 和 books are what draw people in 对应。I 段虽也提到文化场所，但讲的是开放日与音乐会，不涉及营业额构成。"},{"n":43,"stem":"Representatives of late-shift workers secured a paid journey home but are still negotiating over how far ahead schedules are issued.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。该段末三句说保证一点后下班者有车回家已获同意并由夜间执照费出资，而提前一周公布排班仍在谈判，与题干 secured a paid journey home 和 still negotiating 对应。F 段讲的是顾客安全，不是员工待遇。"},{"n":44,"stem":"Sound limits that were enforced consistently cut complaints without reducing the number of late licences.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。该段说划定安静区、十一点后限噪、违者停发夜间执照一个月，结果投诉下降约三分之一，而申请夜间执照的场所反而增多，与题干 cut complaints 和 without reducing the number of late licences 对应。F 段同样涉及治理，但主题是人身安全。"},{"n":45,"stem":"The value of the night economy should be measured by residents' sleep and workers' journeys, not only by sales and jobs.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。该段先列出议会自己的经济指标，再说 Others would add quieter measures，例如护士深夜能否不花钱回家、居民七月能否开窗睡觉，与题干 measured by residents' sleep and workers' journeys 对应。A 段讲的是策略的由来，不是评价标准。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Few arguments about reading produce as much heat as the one about screens. Publishers report that sales of printed books have held up better than anyone predicted, while students arriving at university now carry entire reading lists on a single tablet. The assumption behind much of the enthusiasm is simple: if a text is available instantly, searchable and cheap, then reading it must be at least as effective. A small study at Brookfield College suggests that the assumption needs qualifying.\nThe team asked a hundred and twenty undergraduates to read the same three-thousand-word article, half of them on paper and half on a tablet. Both groups had twenty minutes, and neither group was allowed to take notes. Immediately afterwards the students answered two kinds of question. The first asked them to put six events from the article in the correct order; the second asked where in the text a particular claim had appeared. On the first task the paper group did slightly better. On the second the gap was wide: most of those who had read on paper could say whether a claim came early or late, while many tablet readers could not place it at all.\nThe obvious explanation would be distraction, and the team was careful to rule it out. The tablets were offline, notifications were switched off, and a researcher sat in the room throughout. What differed, the authors argue, was the physical shape of the text. A printed article has thickness: the reader feels how much remains, sees a paragraph at the top of a left-hand page, and remembers that a difficult passage came just before a diagram. Scrolling removes these landmarks. The text becomes a narrow window that never shows where the reader is.\nThe result should not be read as a verdict against screens. The difference in scores was modest, and it shrank to almost nothing among students who read long documents on a tablet every week. Nor did the study test the advantages that digital texts clearly have: a reader can enlarge the type, search for a name, or carry forty books on a journey. For a student checking a date in a report, a screen is faster and better.\nThe practical lesson is about matching the tool to the job. Where the aim is to follow an argument, remember its structure and carry it into an examination, print still has an edge. Where the aim is to find, compare or extract, the screen wins easily. Publishers who care about comprehension might also learn something: keeping page layout stable, and showing readers how far they have travelled, costs little and may recover part of what scrolling takes away.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What were the participants in the study asked to do?","options":{"A":"Read an article and then discuss it with a partner.","B":"Read the same article in two formats and answer questions about it.","C":"Read several articles of different lengths within twenty minutes.","D":"Take notes while reading and hand them in afterwards."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 1 句说研究让 120 名本科生读同一篇三千词文章，一半用纸、一半用平板，随后回答两类问题，B 项是对这一设计的概括。D 项与 neither group was allowed to take notes 相反；C 项与 the same three-thousand-word article 不符。"},{"n":47,"stem":"What did the study find about the tablet readers?","options":{"A":"They recalled the order of events better than the paper readers.","B":"They finished the article more quickly than the paper readers.","C":"They were much less able to say where a claim appeared.","D":"They were more often disturbed by incoming messages."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 2 段末句说在「某说法出现在文中何处」这一任务上 the gap was wide，many tablet readers could not place it at all，C 项即此意。A 项与原文相反，排序题上是纸书组略好；D 项把被排除的原因当成结论，第 3 段已说明设备离线。"},{"n":48,"stem":"Why does the author mention that the tablets were offline?","options":{"A":"To show that the tablets cost less than printed copies.","B":"To explain why the tablet group read more slowly.","C":"To suggest that the study was paid for by a technology firm.","D":"To rule out distraction as the cause of the difference."},"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D。第 3 段开头说最省事的解释是分心，接着讲平板离线、通知关闭、研究员全程在场，The team was careful to rule it out，与 D 项对应。B 项「读得更慢」原文未提；C 项属无依据的猜测。"},{"n":49,"stem":"The word 'landmarks' in paragraph 3 most probably refers to","options":{"A":"features of a page that help a reader remember its layout.","B":"important claims that the author wants readers to notice.","C":"marks that students made on the paper as they read.","D":"headings that divide a long article into sections."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 3 段先说纸页有厚度、读者能感到还剩多少、记得某段在左页顶端，随后说 Scrolling removes these landmarks，可见它指帮助记忆版面位置的纸面特征。C 项与第 2 段不许做笔记矛盾；D 项「小标题」原文未提。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards reading on screens?","options":{"A":"Students preparing for examinations should avoid it entirely.","B":"It suits some purposes but is weaker for deep reading.","C":"It will soon replace printed books in universities.","D":"The evidence against it is strong enough to justify a ban."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 4 段说结果不应被读成对屏幕的判决，差异不大，且数字文本在放大字号、检索、携带上优势明显；第 5 段结论是按用途选格式，B 项最贴合。A、D 两项把它推向禁用，属过度推断；C 项原文无据。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"Every university health service says the same thing in different words: students know that exercise is good for them, and most of them still do not do it. The advice they receive usually stresses the body, with warnings about weight, blood pressure and long hours at a desk. Less often mentioned is the effect on mood, although that may be the benefit students notice first. A brisk walk after a difficult morning often does more for a low mood than an hour of scrolling, and not simply because walking is healthier.\nPart of the effect is chemical. Moderate activity raises the heart rate, increases blood flow to the brain and releases substances that reduce the sensation of pain and, with it, some of the feeling of strain. But chemistry explains less than people assume. Exercise also interrupts rumination: a student who is walking has to watch for traffic, choose a route and keep moving, and the mind has less room to replay the same anxious thought. And there is the plain satisfaction of finishing something, which is why a short session completed is usually worth more than a long one abandoned.\nA study at Brookfield College tested how much of this depends on effort. Ninety students who described themselves as stressed were divided into three groups. One walked briskly for thirty minutes three times a week, one followed a programme of gentle stretching, and one was placed on a waiting list. After eight weeks the walkers reported the largest fall in stress and the best sleep. But the stretching group also improved, by roughly half as much, although their hearts worked far less. Intensity, the authors conclude, is not the only ingredient.\nTwo practical points follow. The first is that ten minutes counts. Students who plan an hour of exercise often do none, while those who accept a ten-minute walk between lectures usually do more than they intended. The second is that daylight matters: a walk outdoors in the morning appears to help sleep more than the same walk under artificial light at midnight. Neither point requires equipment, a membership or a change of clothes.\nNone of this makes exercise a treatment for depression, and the authors are careful to say so. Students whose low mood lasts for weeks, or who stop attending classes, need professional help rather than a running plan. For the ordinary strain of an examination term, however, the evidence is encouraging: a little regular movement, repeated often enough to become a habit, changes how a week feels.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"What does the author say is usually left out of advice about exercise?","options":{"A":"Its effect on body weight.","B":"Its effect on mood.","C":"The time needed to see results.","D":"The risk of injury to beginners."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段第 3 句说通常的建议强调的是身体，Less often mentioned is the effect on mood，B 项即此。A 项恰是常被提到的内容，与题干 left out 相反；C、D 两项原文未涉及。"},{"n":52,"stem":"According to paragraph 2, how does walking help a worried student?","options":{"A":"It leaves the mind less room to repeat the same anxious thought.","B":"It removes the cause of the student's anxiety.","C":"It makes the student too tired to think about problems.","D":"It gives the student a chance to talk the problem over."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 2 段第 4 句说运动会打断 rumination，走路的人要看车、选路线、保持移动，the mind has less room to replay the same anxious thought，A 项是这句的同义改写。B 项说消除焦虑的根源，属夸大；D 项「与人交谈」原文没有。"},{"n":53,"stem":"What was notable about the results of the eight-week study?","options":{"A":"The waiting list group improved as much as the walkers.","B":"The walkers ended up sleeping worse than the other groups.","C":"Students who exercised less often reported lower stress.","D":"Gentle stretching brought about half the gain of brisk walking."},"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D。第 3 段倒数第 2 句说拉伸组也有改善，by roughly half as much，作者据此得出强度不是唯一因素的结论，D 项对应。A 项与步行组降幅最大相矛盾；B 项把 the best sleep 说反。"},{"n":54,"stem":"What does the author mean by saying that ten minutes counts?","options":{"A":"Even a short session is worth doing.","B":"Ten minutes is the minimum needed for any benefit.","C":"Sessions should be timed and written down.","D":"Students should exercise for ten minutes every day."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 4 段在 The first is that ten minutes counts 之后解释：打算练一小时的人常常什么都不做，而接受课间走十分钟的人往往做得比预想多，可见 counts 意为「算数、有价值」。B 项把它读成最低有效时长，原文没有这层意思；D 项「每天十分钟」也非原意。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the main message of the passage?","options":{"A":"Exercise can take the place of professional treatment for depression.","B":"Students should join a gym to cope with examination stress.","C":"Small amounts of regular movement can improve how students feel.","D":"Intensity is the most important factor in relieving stress."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。全文从情绪益处、作用机制、八周实验讲到具体建议，落点在末段 a little regular movement, repeated often enough to become a habit, changes how a week feels，C 项概括最准。A 项与作者明确否认运动可替代治疗相反；D 项与强度不是唯一因素矛盾。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay. Suppose your class is discussing whether college students should be allowed to keep pets in dormitories. Write an essay of 120-180 words stating your opinion and giving reasons.","prompt_cn":"假设你班正在讨论大学生是否可以在宿舍养宠物，请就此写一篇 120–180 词的短文，说明你的观点并给出理由。","word_limit":120,"sample_essay":"Whether students should be allowed to keep pets in their dormitories is a question my class has argued over for a week. I would answer no, though not without sympathy for the other side.\nThe practical objections are hard to dismiss. A room shared by four people is not a private space, and a cat or a rabbit affects every one of them through allergies, night-time noise and smells that no air freshener can hide. Animals also need food, veterinary care and daily company. When the summer holiday comes, a student cannot put a pet in a cupboard, and the animals abandoned near campus each June show what happens when a rule is ignored rather than discussed.\nCompanionship is a real need, but a dormitory is the wrong place to meet it. A small aquarium causes little trouble, and a common room where students walk a shelter dog for an afternoon teaches responsibility without forcing it on roommates. That is a better answer than a rule everyone breaks.","sample_essay_cn":"大学生是否可以在宿舍养宠物，这个问题我们班已经争了一个星期。我的回答是不可以，虽然我也理解另一方的想法。\n现实层面的反对理由很难驳倒。一间四人合住的房间并不是私人空间，一只猫或一只兔子会通过过敏、夜里的响动和空气清新剂遮不住的气味影响到每一个人。动物还需要食物、看病和每天的陪伴。到了暑假，学生总不能把宠物塞进柜子；每年六月校园附近被遗弃的动物，正说明当规则被无视而不是被讨论时会发生什么。\n陪伴是真实的需要，但宿舍不是满足这种需要的合适场所。养一小缸鱼麻烦不大；设一间公共活动室，让学生下午去遛一只收容所的狗，既能教人负责，又不必把责任强加给室友。这比一条人人都违反的规定要好。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"剪纸是中国最古老的民间艺术之一，已有一千五百多年历史。人们用剪刀在红纸上剪出花鸟、人物和吉祥图案，贴在窗户、门楣上，为节日添彩。北方剪纸粗犷有力，南方剪纸细腻秀丽，各地风格迥异。过去，这门手艺由农村妇女口传心授，母女相传；如今它走进课堂和设计工作室，年轻人把纹样用在服装和包装上。二〇〇九年，中国剪纸被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产名录，这门古老手艺因此受到更多关注。","reference_en":"Paper-cutting is one of the oldest folk arts in China, with a history of more than fifteen hundred years. With a pair of scissors or a small knife, people cut flowers, birds, figures and auspicious patterns out of red paper and paste them on windows and door lintels to add to the cheer of a festival. In the north the lines are bold and vigorous, while in the south they are delicate and graceful, and the styles of different regions differ noticeably. In the past the craft was passed on by word of mouth, usually from mother to daughter in the countryside; today it has entered classrooms and design studios, and young people use traditional patterns on clothing and packaging and in animation. In 2009 Chinese paper-cutting was added to the UNESCO list of intangible cultural heritage, and this old craft has since attracted wider attention.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet6-mock-1":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":1,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第1套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"When offices closed in 2020, remote work was treated as a [[26]] arrangement, to be undone within months. Six years later it has become a permanent feature of white-collar employment, and managers continue to [[27]] with its consequences. Early enthusiasts promised that distance would make organizations leaner and faster; the evidence has proved more [[28]] than the slogan allowed, with gains in some roles and losses in others. Consultants who once sold open-plan offices now sell software that watches what happens in the home.\n\nThe most [[29]] change is managerial rather than technological. Supervisors who once measured presence must now measure output, and many regard the new paperwork as a [[30]] rather than a tool. A survey of 1,200 firms by the Halden Institute found that nearly half of managers admitted they had no reliable way to [[31]] the productivity of a team they rarely saw. In the absence of such measures, decisions about promotion tend to [[32]] on visibility rather than results, which in practice quietly rewards those who are most [[33]] in meetings rather than those whose work is strongest.\n\nOrganizations have responded in predictable ways. Some have [[34]] to surveillance software, tracking keystrokes and screen time; others have cut office space and passed the savings to staff. Neither approach touches the underlying [[35]] between autonomy and oversight: trust, once supplied by shared rooms, must now be built deliberately. The firms that manage this well tend to be those that wrote down what they expected, reviewed it openly, and let people choose where to work.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"burden"},{"k":"B","t":"consensus"},{"k":"C","t":"gauge"},{"k":"D","t":"grapple"},{"k":"E","t":"hinge"},{"k":"F","t":"incentive"},{"k":"G","t":"nuanced"},{"k":"H","t":"profound"},{"k":"I","t":"provisional"},{"k":"J","t":"resort"},{"k":"K","t":"rigid"},{"k":"L","t":"scarcely"},{"k":"M","t":"sustain"},{"k":"N","t":"tension"},{"k":"O","t":"vocal"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) provisional。第 1 段第 1 句，空格修饰 arrangement，紧随其后的 to be undone within months 说明这只是暂时安排，provisional 意为临时的、暂定的。K) rigid 意为僵化不变，与随时可以取消相矛盾；G) nuanced 不能修饰 arrangement。"},{"n":27,"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D) grapple。第 1 段第 2 句 continue to ___ with its consequences，只有 grapple 能与 with 搭配表示竭力应对。E) hinge 也接介词，但搭配的是 on；C) gauge 是及物动词，后面不接 with。"},{"n":28,"answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G) nuanced。第 1 段第 3 句 more ___ than the slogan allowed，冒号后的 gains in some roles and losses in others 说明实际情况有得有失、比口号复杂，nuanced 正合此意。H) profound 只强调程度深，无法对应好坏参半；K) rigid 与 more ... than 的比较结构语义不通。"},{"n":29,"answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H) profound。第 2 段第 1 句 The most ___ change is managerial rather than technological，下文说明变化在于衡量方式与权力关系，profound 表示深刻的、根本的。O) vocal 只修饰人或言论；I) provisional 与后文强调的长期性相反。"},{"n":30,"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A) burden。第 2 段第 2 句 regard the new paperwork as a ___ rather than a tool，rather than a tool 提示这里要填一个与工具相对、带贬义的可数名词，burden 意为负担。F) incentive 是褒义，与转折关系不符；B) consensus 表示共识，不能与 a 连用。"},{"n":31,"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C) gauge。第 2 段第 3 句 no reliable way to ___ the productivity，需要及物动词表示衡量、评估，gauge 与 productivity 搭配自然。M) sustain 意为维持，与 no reliable way 的否定语义冲突；D) grapple 不能直接带宾语。"},{"n":32,"answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E) hinge。第 2 段第 4 句 tend to ___ on visibility rather than results，hinge on 意为取决于，与 rather than results 形成对照。J) resort 搭配的是 to；C) gauge 后面不接 on。"},{"n":33,"answer":"O","explanation":"答案 O) vocal。第 2 段第 4 句 rewards those who are most ___ in meetings rather than those whose work is strongest，与 work is strongest 对照，指的是在会上最敢发声的人，vocal 意为敢于直言的。K) rigid 形容人不知变通，与开会发言的语境无关。"},{"n":34,"answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J) resort。第 3 段第 2 句 Some have ___ to surveillance software，resort to 意为诉诸、采用（常含别无他法之意），与后文 another group 的对照写法一致。E) hinge 接 on 不接 to；M) sustain 是及物动词，后面不能跟 to。"},{"n":35,"answer":"N","explanation":"答案 N) tension。第 3 段第 3 句 the underlying ___ between autonomy and oversight，between 结构要求一个表示两者对立关系的名词，tension 意为张力、矛盾。F) incentive 无法与 between 搭配表达对立；A) burden 是单方面的负担，不含双方拉扯之意。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For two decades museums have promised that digitisation would throw their doors open to anyone with a screen. The promise is easy to sell: a collection that once required a flight and a ticket can now be visited from a bedroom. Yet the phrase 'virtual museum' conceals as much as it reveals, because what appears online is never the institution itself but a selection made by people with budgets, deadlines and reputations to protect. The interesting questions are therefore not about whether museums should go digital, since they already have, but about what happens to authority, ownership and attention once the gallery walls stop doing the work of organising a visit."},{"label":"B","text":"The technical side is less glamorous than the results suggest. A single painting may be photographed hundreds of times under carefully controlled lighting and then stitched together by software into an image of several billion pixels. Conservators are usually present, because moving a fragile canvas can do real damage, and colour must be checked against a physical reference chart or the digital copy will drift. A large museum might complete a few hundred objects a year this way, which sounds impressive until it is set against a collection of several million. Selection, not equipment, remains the binding constraint."},{"label":"C","text":"Objects that resist flat photography, such as carved stone, ceramic vessels and costumes, are increasingly captured with structured light or photogrammetry, producing models that a viewer can rotate and measure. The results can be startlingly intimate: you can see tool marks that a display case would never let you approach. But a scan records surfaces, not significance. It cannot convey weight, the smell of old lacquer, or the fact that an object was repaired three times by the same family. Curators describe these models as excellent evidence and poor substitutes, useful for study and misleading as atmosphere."},{"label":"D","text":"Putting a hundred objects online is cataloguing; arranging them into an argument is curating. The most ambitious virtual exhibitions borrow the grammar of film rather than the grammar of the gallery: a fixed sequence, a narrator, a soundtrack, a point of view that cannot be argued with by wandering off. Visitors gain interpretation and lose the accidental encounter, the wrong turn that leads to an unexpected object. Designers who understand this often build in deliberate detours, but a screen rewards efficiency in a way that a building does not, and most people simply follow the arrow."},{"label":"E","text":"Digital visitors leave traces that physical ones never did. Platforms record how long a page stays open, where the cursor pauses, which objects are shared and which are abandoned after two seconds. This is genuinely useful: it can show that a wall of text is being skipped or that a video is watched to the end. The risk is that metrics of attention become a proxy for value, and that the works which hold a viewer longest, the spectacular, the cute and the mildly scandalous, quietly set the agenda for what gets scanned next."},{"label":"F","text":"Rights are the least romantic and most decisive constraint. A museum may own a painting and still lack the right to publish a high-resolution image of it, because copyright in the work may sit with an artist's estate, and photographs of three-dimensional objects attract their own protections in some jurisdictions. Works whose owners cannot be traced are the hardest case: the cost of a failed search can exceed the value of publication, so they stay offline. What looks like curatorial caution is often legal arithmetic."},{"label":"G","text":"Digitisation also redistributes authorship. Once a collection is online, the labels, the ordering and the metadata become the museum's public voice, and they are read by audiences who may never see the building. Cataloguers who once wrote for specialists now write for anyone who arrives from a search engine. Some institutions have responded by publishing their data openly and inviting outside scholars to annotate it, which produces richer records and looser control. Others keep the record closed, arguing that interpretation is a form of authority that should not be outsourced to whoever is loudest."},{"label":"H","text":"The strongest argument for the digital museum is not novelty but reach. A school without the budget for a coach trip can spend an afternoon in a gallery three thousand miles away; a visitor who cannot manage stairs or crowds can examine a manuscript at their own pace, at two in the morning, with the text enlarged. Surveys of online users consistently find that a substantial minority would never have come in person. That does not make a website a substitute for a building, but it does mean the audience is genuinely larger rather than merely relocated."},{"label":"I","text":"Preservation is usually offered as the reason to scan, and it is a good one: a digital record survives fire, flood and handling. But digital objects are not free to keep. Files need migration as formats fall out of use, storage must be paid for annually, and documentation has to travel with the data or the images become unattributable. Institutions that budgeted for a project but not for its maintenance have already lost material to dead links and unreadable discs. Conservation, it turns out, has acquired a second and far less visible electricity bill."},{"label":"J","text":"None of this argues for retreat. It argues for honesty about what a screen can carry. A museum online is best understood as a second edition rather than a copy: edited, selective, shaped by the constraints of its medium, and worth reading on its own terms. The institutions that do this well are candid about what has been left out, publish their selection criteria, and treat the website as a place to think rather than a shop window. Visitors respond to that candour; they are, after all, quite used to being told that a photograph is not the thing itself."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"Records of how people behave on a museum's website may end up influencing which objects are chosen for future digitisation.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。该段第 3 至 4 句说 attention 的数据容易成为价值的替代指标，于是最吸引眼球的作品 quietly set the agenda for what gets scanned next，与题干 chosen for future digitisation 对应。B 段讲的是拍摄流程与人力限制，不涉及观众行为数据。"},{"n":37,"stem":"A museum may legally own an object and yet be unable to publish a detailed image of it.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。该段第 2 句明确指出博物馆可以 own a painting 却 still lack the right to publish a high-resolution image，原因是著作权可能属于艺术家遗产。C 段讨论三维扫描能记录什么，与权利归属无关。"},{"n":38,"stem":"Three-dimensional scanning captures the outward appearance of an object but not the physical and historical qualities that give it meaning.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。该段第 3 句 a scan records surfaces, not significance，随后列举重量、气味与修补经历等无法传达的信息，正是题干所说的 physical and historical qualities。E 段虽也提到扫描，但焦点是选题依据而非扫描的局限。"},{"n":39,"stem":"How many objects a museum can digitise each year is decided by what has to be left out rather than by the equipment available.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。该段结尾说一年只能完成几百件，而馆藏有数百万件，因此 Selection, not equipment, remains the binding constraint，与题干 decided by what has to be left out 同义。A 段谈的是线上呈现的整体取舍，不涉及年度数量。"},{"n":40,"stem":"Virtual exhibitions that impose a single route offer viewers interpretation but take away the chance of stumbling on something unexpected.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。该段第 2 至 3 句说这类展览借用电影的语法，观众 gain interpretation and lose the accidental encounter，并解释所谓意外就是走错路遇到意料之外的作品。J 段讲线上展览应被看作独立版本，不涉及路线设计。"},{"n":41,"stem":"For a significant number of online visitors, the digital collection is the only realistic means of access.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。该段第 3 句 Surveys ... find that a substantial minority would never have come in person，即这部分人本来根本不会到馆，线上是唯一可行途径。G 段讲线上读者群扩大带来的写作对象变化，不是可达性问题。"},{"n":42,"stem":"Keeping digital copies usable over time demands continuous spending that projects often fail to anticipate.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。该段第 2 至 4 句列举格式迁移、年度存储费用与随数据附带的说明，并指出只给项目立项、不给维护拨款的机构已经丢失资料，对应题干 continuous spending 与 fail to anticipate。B 段提到成本，但指的是拍摄阶段的人力。"},{"n":43,"stem":"When collections move online, the descriptions written by museum staff reach readers who are far less specialised than before.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。该段第 2 句 Cataloguers who once wrote for specialists now write for anyone who arrives from a search engine，说明写作对象由专家变为普通检索者。D 段讲展览叙事，不涉及说明文字的读者变化。"},{"n":44,"stem":"An online collection should be judged as a separate, edited work rather than as a replacement for the physical one.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。该段第 3 句把线上博物馆称为 a second edition rather than a copy，并说它 edited, selective，worth reading on its own terms，与题干 separate, edited work 对应。H 段虽然也说网站不能取代建筑，但结论落在受众扩大上。"},{"n":45,"stem":"The label 'virtual museum' is misleading because what is displayed reflects institutional decisions rather than the collection as a whole.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。该段第 3 句说 'virtual museum' conceals as much as it reveals，线上看到的不是机构本身，而是由预算与截止日期决定的一份 selection，与题干 reflects institutional decisions 对应。F 段谈的是法律限制造成的删减，范围更窄。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Every recommendation system begins with a reasonable premise: that a person's past choices say something about their future preferences. The premise works well for music and shopping, where a wrong guess costs little. Applied to news, it takes on a different character. A system trained to maximise engagement learns, quite accurately, that material confirming an existing belief is clicked more reliably than material that complicates it. The algorithm is not partisan; it is merely obedient to the only signal it has been given.\n\nThe outcome is often described as an echo chamber, though the metaphor is imprecise. In a true echo chamber a person hears only their own voice returned. What recommendation systems produce is closer to a hall of mirrors, in which many voices speak but all of them agree. Exposure to a narrow range of opinion may not even be the main mechanism: several large studies suggest that self-selection, what people choose when nobody is steering, accounts for more of the narrowing than the ranking itself. The uncomfortable implication is that the machine is amplifying a tendency we supplied.\n\nThe attention economy sharpens the effect. When revenue depends on minutes watched, content that provokes is worth more than content that informs, because indignation travels faster than nuance. Outlets learn this quickly, and the vocabulary of public argument adjusts: headlines are written for the click rather than the reader, and qualifications that would slow a sentence down are cut. Readers then meet a world that looks more hostile than it is, and the belief that everyone else is unreasonable becomes a fair inference from the evidence they are shown.\n\nRemedies tend to be aimed at the wrong layer. Labels, fact-check tags and warnings sit on individual items, while the distortion happens in the aggregate, in what is never shown. A feed can be accurate item by item and still leave a reader with a false picture of the whole. Some platforms have experimented with deliberate randomness, injecting a small proportion of unfamiliar sources; early results suggest that it widens exposure modestly without destroying engagement. It is a small lever, but it works on the structure rather than the symptom.\n\nThe deeper difficulty is that any remedy requires someone to decide what a healthy information diet looks like, and that decision is itself a form of editorial power. Regulators are uneasy about making it, platforms are unwilling, and users, asked directly, tend to say they want balance and then click on outrage. A serious response will have to begin by admitting that the problem is not a technical fault to be patched but a set of choices about whose judgement should shape what millions of people see.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What does the author say about recommendation systems applied to news?","options":{"A":"They were built by engineers who wanted to promote one side of a debate.","B":"They work as reliably for news as they do for music and shopping.","C":"They pursue engagement without intending to favour any particular viewpoint.","D":"They predict news preferences less accurately than shopping habits."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 1 段最后一句 The algorithm is not partisan; it is merely obedient to the only signal it has been given，说明系统只是服从点击这一信号，并无立场。A 项与 not partisan 直接相反；B 项偷换概念，原文说同一前提用于新闻 it takes on a different character。"},{"n":47,"stem":"By the image of a hall of mirrors, the author means that users","options":{"A":"hear their own opinions repeated back to them.","B":"meet a wide range of voices that nevertheless share one outlook.","C":"can no longer recognise their own reflection.","D":"have far fewer sources available than they once did."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 3 句 many voices speak but all of them agree，镜厅的特点是声音多而立场一致。A 项是上一句对真正回音室的定义，作者明确说这个比喻 imprecise，故不能选；D 项谈数量减少，与 many voices 矛盾。"},{"n":48,"stem":"What do several large studies indicate about the narrowing of exposure?","options":{"A":"Ranking algorithms are its dominant cause.","B":"People's own choices contribute more to it than ranking does.","C":"Users actively seek out views that challenge their own.","D":"It results mainly from limited access to news sources."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 4 句 self-selection ... accounts for more of the narrowing than the ranking itself，即自我选择的作用大于排序机制。A 项把主次颠倒；C 项与 self-selection 导致收窄的结论相反。"},{"n":49,"stem":"Why does the author think current remedies fail to address the real problem?","options":{"A":"Because most individual items in a feed are inaccurate.","B":"Because the damage lies in what is left out rather than in single items.","C":"Because regulators have refused to take any action.","D":"Because platforms remove warnings too quickly."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 4 段第 1 至 2 句说标签与警告都贴在单条内容上，而 distortion happens in the aggregate, in what is never shown。A 项与 A feed can be accurate item by item 相反；C 项把 regulators are uneasy 夸大为拒绝行动。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What is the author's point in the final paragraph?","options":{"A":"Better engineering will eventually remove the distortion.","B":"Users should be allowed to design their own feeds.","C":"Any remedy rests on contestable judgments about whose standards count.","D":"Regulation is the only acceptable way forward."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 5 段第 1 句说任何补救都要有人判定什么算健康的资讯结构，而这一判定本身就是 editorial power，末句又强调问题是一组关于谁的判断该起作用的选择。A 项与 not a technical fault to be patched 相反；D 项与 Regulators are uneasy about making it 不符。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"Few phrases do as much work in public life as 'studies show'. It appears in headlines, advertising copy and ministerial speeches, and it carries an authority that the underlying research rarely claims. A single experiment on forty undergraduates and a meta-analysis of two hundred trials can be reported in identical language, and readers have no way of telling them apart. The phrase is not a lie; it is a compression, and like every compression it discards precisely the information that would let a reader judge how much weight to give the claim.\n\nScientists are trained to express doubt and journalists are trained to remove it. A paper concluding that an effect 'may be present under certain conditions' reaches a phone screen as a finding that a substance 'does' something. The transformation is not usually dishonest. Hedging is expensive in a headline, and a sentence listing three caveats reads as evasion to an audience accustomed to assertion. The result, though, is a public that meets certainty where researchers intended a probability.\n\nThis creates a peculiar trap. When later work fails to replicate an earlier result, the public concludes that scientists cannot be trusted, having been told for years that the matter was settled. Researchers, in turn, become more guarded in public, which is read as further evidence of confusion. The cycle punishes the honest and rewards the confident. Surveys in several countries find that trust in scientists as a group remains comparatively high even as trust in particular findings falls, a pattern suggesting that people are not rejecting expertise so much as losing confidence in its stability.\n\nSome communicators have responded by making uncertainty explicit instead of hiding it. Rather than delete the hedge, they translate it: this study found a small effect in one group, and we do not yet know whether it holds elsewhere. Early trials of the approach produced a surprise. Readers did not lose confidence; they reported more of it, because the message now contained information they could use. What damages credibility is not admitted doubt but doubt that surfaces only after the fact.\n\nNone of this implies that audiences are incapable of handling complexity. It implies that they have rarely been offered it in a form that respects their time. The practical lesson for anyone writing about research is unglamorous: say what was measured, in whom, and how firmly, and resist the sentence that sounds decisive. A claim that arrives with its limits attached is harder to misrepresent, and much harder to abandon quietly later.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"What point does the author make about the phrase 'studies show' in paragraph 1?","options":{"A":"It is deliberately used to deceive ordinary readers.","B":"It lends a claim more authority than the research behind it supports.","C":"It appears mainly in commercial advertising rather than in news.","D":"It usefully distinguishes strong evidence from weak evidence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段第 2 句 it carries an authority that the underlying research rarely claims，即措辞给出的权威高于研究本身。A 项与 The phrase is not a lie 相反；D 项与第 3 句 forty undergraduates 与 two hundred trials 被同样表述的事实相反。"},{"n":52,"stem":"The word 'compression' (Para. 1) refers to","options":{"A":"shortening a text so that it fits into a headline.","B":"the loss of the details that would allow a claim to be weighed.","C":"combining many separate trials into one analysis.","D":"reporting fewer participants than a study actually had."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段末句 it discards precisely the information that would let a reader judge how much weight to give the claim，压缩的关键在于丢掉判断依据。A 项只看到字数缩短这一表层；C 项是 meta-analysis 的定义，属于偷换概念。"},{"n":53,"stem":"Why does hedging tend to disappear on its way to the public?","options":{"A":"Because researchers exaggerate their findings on purpose.","B":"Because a qualified statement looks evasive to readers who expect assertion.","C":"Because editors are required to delete it by their employers.","D":"Because the original papers seldom contain any hedges."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段第 3 至 4 句说 Hedging is expensive in a headline，列举限定的句子在习惯断言的读者眼里 reads as evasion。A 项与 The transformation is not usually dishonest 相反；D 项与 Scientists are trained to express doubt 相反。"},{"n":54,"stem":"What does the survey pattern described in paragraph 3 suggest?","options":{"A":"The public has stopped trusting scientists altogether.","B":"Declining faith in particular findings is not a rejection of expertise.","C":"Researchers should avoid speaking to the public at all.","D":"Replication studies are less dependable than original ones."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段末句说对科学家群体的信任仍较高而对具体结论的信任下降，可见人们 not rejecting expertise so much as losing confidence in its stability。A 项与 comparatively high 相反；C 项属于过度推断，原文只说研究者变得更谨慎。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the author's main argument in the last two paragraphs?","options":{"A":"Audiences are unable to cope with statistical detail.","B":"Admitting uncertainty can raise public confidence rather than lower it.","C":"Journalists should stop reporting the results of single studies.","D":"Scientists should avoid using words such as 'may'."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 4 段第 3 至 4 句 Readers did not lose confidence; they reported more of it，第 5 段进一步说读者缺的是尊重其时间的表达方式。A 项与 None of this implies that audiences are incapable 相反；D 项与全文主张保留限定语相反。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on whether artificial intelligence will replace creative work. You should state your opinion and support it with reasons. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.","prompt_cn":"以「人工智能是否会取代创造性工作」为题写一篇短文，说明你的观点并给出理由。","word_limit":150,"sample_essay":"Whether artificial intelligence will replace creative work is a question usually answered with either panic or complacency, and neither is useful. The honest reply is that it will replace some of it, absorb more, and leave a stubborn remainder it cannot touch.\n\nWhat machines do well is variation at speed. Given a brief, they produce a hundred competent designs, melodies or paragraphs before a human has sharpened a pencil. This is disruptive for work that was always repetitive: stock images, routine jingles, the fourth revision of a press release. But the value of such output lies in its adequacy rather than its originality, and adequacy is what a market pays least for.\n\nThe remainder is harder to automate because it is not a product but a judgement: knowing which of the hundred options matters, and why. Taste is formed by failure, by argument, and by caring about something beyond the brief. A model can imitate the surface of that care; it cannot have a stake in it.\n\nThe likely future is therefore not replacement but a sharper division of labour, in which machines supply options and people supply reasons.","sample_essay_cn":"人工智能是否会取代创造性工作，这个问题通常得到的回答要么是恐慌，要么是自满，而这两种都没有用处。诚实的回答是：它会取代其中一部分，吞并更多的一部分，并留下一块它碰不到的顽固残余。\n\n机器擅长的是快速生成变体。给出一份需求说明，它们能在人还没削好铅笔之前拿出上百个还算合格的设计、旋律或段落。这对本来就重复的劳动确实是冲击：通用图库、套路化的广告配乐、新闻稿的第四稿。但这类产出的价值在于够用而非原创，而「够用」恰恰是市场付钱最少的品质。\n\n剩下的那部分难以自动化，因为它不是产品而是判断：知道上百个方案里哪一个要紧，以及为什么。品味由失败、由与人争论、由在意需求之外的东西塑造而成。模型可以模仿这份在意的表面，却无法真正与之利害相关。\n\n因此未来更可能不是取代，而是分工更细：机器提供选项，人提供理由。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"二十四节气是中国古人根据太阳在一年中的位置变化而划分的时间体系，用来指导农事与日常生活。它把一年分为二十四个阶段，每段约十五天，并各配一个简洁的名称，如立春、清明、霜降。这些名称记录了气温、降水与动植物变化的规律，至今仍影响着耕作安排和饮食习俗。2016 年，这一体系被正式列入人类非物质文化遗产名录。今天，许多城市居民依然会按节气调整饮食，把它看作与自然保持联系的一种方式。","reference_en":"The twenty-four solar terms form a system of time that the ancient Chinese devised from the changing position of the sun over the course of a year, and they were used to guide farming and daily life. The system divides the year into twenty-four periods of about fifteen days each, giving every period a concise name such as Start of Spring, Clear and Bright, or Descent of Frost. These names record patterns of temperature, rainfall and the behaviour of plants and animals, and they still shape when fields are worked and what people eat. In 2016 the system was formally added to the list of humanity's intangible cultural heritage. Today many city dwellers still adjust their diet according to the terms, regarding them as a way of keeping in touch with the natural world.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet6-mock-2":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":2,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第2套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"On a summer afternoon a dense city can run several degrees warmer than the farmland beyond its edge. Known as the urban heat island effect, the gap is not simply a matter of latitude. Concrete, asphalt and dark roofing [[26]] sunlight by day and release it slowly after sunset, so streets stay warm long into the night. Air conditioners then work harder and [[27]] more electricity, pushing outdoor temperatures higher.\n\nPlanners now treat it as a design problem, not an inevitable one. Green roofs planted with low-growing species [[28]] the sun's energy for evaporation instead of storing it, and they insulate the building beneath. Permeable paving lets rain [[29]] into the soil rather than run off into drains, keeping surfaces cooler and easing the load on sewers during storms. Street trees cast shade over pavements and [[30]] moisture through their leaves.\n\nNone of these measures is expensive in itself, but their [[31]] depends on scale. A single planted roof cools only the floor below it; a network of roofs and shaded streets can lower temperatures across a district. Cities with such networks report the effect is most [[32]] in the evening, when the day's heat would otherwise be released.\n\nCritics note that green infrastructure demands [[33]] maintenance, and trees need years to mature. Yet the alternatives—larger air-conditioning plants, wider roads and more powerful pumps—are [[34]] more costly to build and to run, and in the long run they tend to [[35]] the very problem they were meant to solve, since every extra unit of cooling adds heat to the street.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"absorb"},{"k":"B","t":"benefit"},{"k":"C","t":"capture"},{"k":"D","t":"considerably"},{"k":"E","t":"constant"},{"k":"F","t":"consume"},{"k":"G","t":"expense"},{"k":"H","t":"gradual"},{"k":"I","t":"impact"},{"k":"J","t":"pronounced"},{"k":"K","t":"roughly"},{"k":"L","t":"release"},{"k":"M","t":"soak"},{"k":"N","t":"capacity"},{"k":"O","t":"aggravate"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A) absorb。主语是 Concrete, asphalt and dark roofing 三个并列名词，谓语须用复数现在时，且与后半句 release it slowly 形成对照，故选 absorb（吸收）。F) consume 虽是动词，但宾语通常是燃料或电力，与 sunlight 搭配不当。"},{"n":27,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) consume。and 连接 work harder 与另一个并列谓语，主语 Air conditioners 为复数，须用现在时原形；consume electricity 是固定搭配，后文 pushing outdoor temperatures higher 也说明用电量增加。A) absorb 与 electricity 不能搭配。"},{"n":28,"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C) capture。主语 Green roofs 为复数，须用现在时动词；capture the sun's energy for evaporation 与 instead of storing it 构成对比，强调截获而非储存。L) release 与 instead of storing 语义方向相反。"},{"n":29,"answer":"M","explanation":"答案 M) soak。lets rain ___ into the soil 中 lets 后接动词原形，soak into 意为渗入，与 rather than run off into drains 形成对照。C) capture 不能与 into the soil 搭配，语义也不通。"},{"n":30,"answer":"L","explanation":"答案 L) release。and 连接 cast shade 与另一个谓语，主语 Street trees 为复数；树木通过叶片释放水分，与遮阴并列说明行道树的作用。M) soak 与 through their leaves 搭配不当。"},{"n":31,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) impact。their 后须接名词，depends on scale 说明讨论的是作用的大小，且与下文 the effect is most ... 呼应。G) expense 与 depends on scale 逻辑不通，本段也不是在谈费用。"},{"n":32,"answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J) pronounced。is most ___ 需要形容词作表语，most 前无 the，说明此处是程度副词修饰形容词；pronounced 意为明显，与傍晚释放蓄热的语境一致。H) gradual 与傍晚最明显这一判断相矛盾。"},{"n":33,"answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E) constant。demands ___ maintenance 中需要形容词修饰 maintenance，constant 表示持续不断的，与后文树木需多年长成共同说明维护负担。H) gradual 修饰 maintenance 时无法表达频率，语义不成立。"},{"n":34,"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D) considerably。are ___ more costly 中 more 前须用副词修饰比较级，considerably 表示大大地。K) roughly 与 more costly 连用意为大约更贵，逻辑上说不通。"},{"n":35,"answer":"O","explanation":"答案 O) aggravate。tend to 后接动词原形，宾语 the very problem they were meant to solve 说明这些替代方案反而加剧了问题。C) capture 与 problem 不能搭配。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"The arithmetic of ageing is simple and unforgiving. In many countries the number of people over eighty is growing faster than any other age group, while the number of adults of working age who might look after them is not. Families are smaller, adult children often settle in another city, and more women, who once supplied most unpaid care, now hold full-time jobs. The result is a gap that no single institution can close. Debates about care therefore turn less on whether the arrangements must be reorganised than on which combination of them can carry the load."},{"label":"B","text":"Home care is the option most older people say they prefer, and it is usually the cheapest. A visit may involve help with washing, dressing, medication and a hot meal, and it allows someone to remain among familiar neighbours. Yet the model has structural weaknesses. Visits are often fifteen or thirty minutes long, which is enough for tasks but not for conversation. Workers travel between appointments on their own time, and a single absence can leave a frail client without support for a whole day. Recruitment is further hampered by the isolation of the job."},{"label":"C","text":"Day centres occupy a middle ground. Older people arrive in the morning and return home in the afternoon, receiving a meal, exercise classes and physiotherapy in between. The benefit is twofold: clients escape isolation, and relatives who provide most of the daily care gain a few hours of relief. Such centres, however, depend on minibuses and on premises close to where people live, and both are expensive to sustain. Where municipal budgets for transport have been cut, attendance has fallen sharply even though the centres themselves stayed open."},{"label":"D","text":"Long-term care insurance attempts to answer the question of payment. Contributions are collected from workers, sometimes matched by employers, and pooled in a fund that releases money once an independent assessment confirms that a person needs help. Some schemes pay cash, allowing families to reward a relative; others pay providers directly. Cash is popular but hard to monitor, since money intended for care may be spent on something else. Schemes that pay providers keep standards higher but cost more to administer, and the eligibility thresholds are invariably contested."},{"label":"E","text":"Shared living has emerged as an unexpected solution. Universities and housing associations match students with older homeowners who have a spare room; the student offers company and light help in exchange for low rent. Evaluations report high satisfaction on both sides, together with genuine friction over noise, cooking smells and incompatible ideas of tidiness. Schemes that succeed invest heavily in matching and in a named coordinator whom either party can telephone. Those that simply hand over a key tend to collapse within a year, often after a single quarrel about the bathroom."},{"label":"F","text":"None of these arrangements functions without people willing to do the work. Paid care is physically demanding, emotionally draining and, in most places, poorly paid. Staff turnover is high, and agencies spend heavily on recruitment only to lose new employees within months. The remedy most often proposed is a career structure: recognised qualifications, progression into specialist roles, and wages that reflect responsibility. Where such structures exist, vacancies fall. Where care is treated as unskilled work that anyone can pick up, shortages persist regardless of how much public money is spent."},{"label":"G","text":"Technology is frequently offered as a substitute for labour. Sensors can detect a fall, a kettle left on the hob or a front door opened at three in the morning, and alerts can be routed to a relative or a call centre. Video calls ease loneliness for those able to use them. The difficulty is that the people most in need are often the least connected: they may lack devices, broadband or the confidence to learn. A sensor network is a useful supplement to human contact and never a replacement for it."},{"label":"H","text":"Behind every scheme lies a question of fairness. Care for the very old is consumed by one generation and paid for by another, and the arrangement is politically fragile because the benefits are visible immediately while the costs persist for decades. Some argue that insurance is fairest, since everyone contributes and everyone may one day claim. Others reply that general taxation spreads the burden more widely and avoids excluding those who never worked formally. No government has yet found a way to raise the money without a prolonged argument."},{"label":"I","text":"Rural areas expose the weaknesses of every model. Populations are sparse, so a care worker may drive forty minutes between clients and the cost of each visit rises accordingly. Day centres close for lack of numbers, shops and clinics withdraw, and the residents who remain depend on neighbours who are themselves elderly. Informal networks can be remarkably resilient, but they are invisible to funding formulas that count registered users. Mobile services, which bring a doctor or a physiotherapist to a village hall once a week, are among the few responses that work."},{"label":"J","text":"What the evidence supports is not a single model but a portfolio. Home visits, day centres, shared housing and insurance each address part of the problem, and each fails when asked to carry the whole of it. The test of a care system is therefore not which arrangement it prefers but whether older people can move between them as their needs change, and whether the choice is genuinely theirs. Dignity, in practice, means being able to say what kind of help you want and being believed."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"Care may be paid for from pooled contributions and released only after an official assessment of need.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。该段说明长期护理保险的运作：工人缴费、雇主可能配比，资金汇入基金，经独立评估确认当事人需要照护后才放款，与题干 pooled contributions 和 official assessment 对应。B 段讨论的是上门探访的时长问题，不涉及筹资与评估。"},{"n":37,"stem":"Short appointments leave no room for company, and a missed visit can strand a frail person for a day.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。该段指出探访常只有十五到三十分钟，够完成任务却不够交谈，护理员在预约之间自行赶路，一次缺勤就能让体弱者整天无人照应，与题干两处细节一一对应。C 段的日间中心是集体场所，不涉及上门探访。"},{"n":38,"stem":"What decides the fate of shared housing is careful pairing and someone to mediate, not the room itself.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。该段说成功的共居项目在配对和指定协调人上投入很大，而只把钥匙交出去的项目往往一年内就散伙，正是配对与调解比房间本身更关键之意。D 段谈的是保险给付方式，与配对无关。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Monitoring devices help those already equipped to use them and cannot stand in for human presence.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。该段指出最需要技术帮助的人往往最缺乏设备、宽带和学习信心，末句明确说传感器网络只是人际接触的补充，永远不能取代它，与题干两句判断吻合。F 段谈的是护理人力短缺，不是技术。"},{"n":40,"stem":"Formulas that count registered users fail to see the help neighbours give one another in remote places.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。该段说农村的非正式互助网络相当有韧性，却对按登记用户数拨款的公式而言是看不见的，即统计口径漏掉了这部分互助。H 段讨论代际公平，不涉及拨款公式。"},{"n":41,"stem":"Attendance can decline even when a centre stays open, if getting there becomes too costly.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。该段末句说交通预算被削减后，即便中心本身仍然开放，到场人数也大幅下降，与题干的条件关系完全对应。B 段的困难在于探访时间短，不在交通。"},{"n":42,"stem":"Staff shortages ease where care work offers recognised qualifications and visible promotion.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。该段把解决办法落在职业结构上：被认可的资格、进入专科岗位的通道、与责任相称的报酬，并说有这样的结构空缺就下降，与题干同义。E 段讲的是学生与老人的共居安排。"},{"n":43,"stem":"The supply of unpaid care inside families is shrinking as households shrink and more women take paid jobs.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。该段说家庭规模变小、成年子女多迁往他城、原本承担大部分无偿照护的女性更多进入全职工作，照护供给因此萎缩，与题干三点对应。J 段是全文总结，没有涉及人口结构。"},{"n":44,"stem":"The politics of care is awkward because the gains are felt at once while the bill lasts for decades.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。该段说照护的好处当下即可见，成本却要持续数十年，因此这一安排政治上脆弱，与题干 gains are felt at once 和 bill lasts for decades 对应。D 段只讲保险如何运作，未讨论政治难度。"},{"n":45,"stem":"A system should be judged by whether people can switch between arrangements as their circumstances change.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。该段主张照护体系应是多种方式的组合，评判标准不是偏好哪一种，而是老人能否随需求变化在各安排之间转换，与题干 switch between arrangements 对应。F 段谈的是护理人员的职业发展路径。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that between half and ninety per cent will lose their last fluent speaker within a century. The figures are disputed, since the boundary between a language and a dialect is itself a political question, but the direction is not in doubt. What is less often noticed is that a language rarely dies suddenly. It retreats, first from public life, then from the schoolroom, then from the kitchen, until the only people who speak it fluently learned it as children sixty years ago.\n\nLinguists who set out to record such a language face a difficulty that laboratory scientists rarely meet: the object of study is a conversation, and conversations cannot be summoned on demand. A fieldworker may spend months learning enough of a language to ask a question, and years more before speakers are willing to talk freely in front of a microphone. The most valuable material often arrives sideways — a joke, a quarrel, a recipe — when the recorder is running but nobody is performing. Consent, too, must be negotiated repeatedly, because a story told to one researcher may not be intended for an archive that anyone can open.\n\nEven a thorough record is a poor substitute for a living language. A dictionary and a corpus of texts preserve words and some of their uses, but they cannot capture the speed with which a speaker chooses one honorific over another, or the way a particular family pronounces a vowel. Grammars tend to describe the careful speech of the oldest consultants, precisely the variety that younger people no longer hear around them. Documentation, in other words, produces an archive, not a community of speakers.\n\nThis is why a growing number of projects are designed by the communities themselves rather than by visiting academics. In one programme, elders were paid to spend afternoons with teenagers, recording stories and then transcribing them together; the transcripts became school materials, and the recordings the basis of a telephone application. The linguist's role shrank to training and advice. Where such programmes have lasted, the decisive factor has been less money than continuity — the same few people turning up, week after week, for years.\n\nNone of this guarantees survival. A language can be documented exhaustively and still fall silent, and a revival can produce a generation of semi-speakers who are fluent in the classroom register and lost in the kitchen. What has changed is the assumption behind the work. The question is no longer what outsiders can extract from a vanishing community, but what that community decides it wants to keep, and on what terms it is willing to share it.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What does the author emphasise about the way languages disappear?","options":{"A":"They vanish abruptly once the last elderly speaker dies.","B":"They withdraw gradually from one area of daily use to another.","C":"They are lost mainly because schools stop teaching them.","D":"They decline only after written records have ceased."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段第 4 至 5 句说语言很少突然消亡，而是先退出公共生活，再退出课堂，最后退出厨房，即一层层退守，与 B 项逐域退缩同义。A 项说最后一位老人去世便骤然消失，与 rarely dies suddenly 相反；C 项把学校绝对化为唯一原因，原文只把它列为退守的一站。"},{"n":47,"stem":"Why is fieldwork on endangered languages described as unusually demanding?","options":{"A":"Researchers have to master several unrelated languages at once.","B":"Recordings made in the field are usually of poor technical quality.","C":"Natural speech cannot be produced to order, so researchers must wait and earn trust.","D":"Communities generally refuse to permit any recording at all."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 2 段第 1 句说研究对象是对话，而对话无法按需召唤；第 2 句说研究者要花数月乃至数年，等到对方愿意对着麦克风自如说话；第 4 句又说同意须反复协商，合起来即不能点单、只能等待并取得信任。A 项同时掌握数门无关语言原文未提；D 项与文中确有录音材料相矛盾。"},{"n":48,"stem":"The phrase 'arrives sideways' (Para. 2) most probably means that valuable material comes","options":{"A":"from moments the researcher did not plan or solicit.","B":"through written documents rather than speech.","C":"from neighbouring communities instead of the village.","D":"only after long and formal interviews."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 2 段第 3 句在 sideways 之后用破折号列举 a joke, a quarrel, a recipe，并补充录音机开着却无人在表演，说明最有价值的语料往往不是按计划问出来的，而是偶然撞上的。B 项理解为书面材料、C 项理解为邻村来源，都与破折号后的举例不符。"},{"n":49,"stem":"What does the author imply about grammars built on the speech of the oldest consultants?","options":{"A":"They exaggerate the differences between related dialects.","B":"They describe a variety that is already fading from everyday use.","C":"They are more reliable than dictionaries of the same language.","D":"They are compiled by speakers rather than by trained linguists."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 3 段第 3 句说语法书倾向于描写最年长发音人的审慎语体，而这恰恰是年轻人在身边已听不到的变体，暗示这类语法记录的是正在退出日常生活的形式。A 项谈方言差异、C 项比较词典的可靠性，原文都未作此判断。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What change in the practice of language work does the author identify?","options":{"A":"Communities now value documentation more than survival.","B":"Linguists have abandoned fieldwork in favour of archives.","C":"The work is increasingly shaped by what communities choose to keep and share.","D":"Outsiders are no longer welcome in community language projects."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。末段最后一句把问题从外来者能从消失的社区提取什么，转为社区自己决定保留什么、以何种条件分享，重心落在社区的选择权上。A 项与第 5 段首句记录并不保证存续相反；B 项语言学家放弃田野属过度推断，第 4 段只说他退到培训与顾问的位置。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"The gig economy was sold as freedom. Instead of a single employer and a fixed shift, a worker would log on when it suited, accept the jobs that appealed, and be paid for each one. Platforms described themselves as marketplaces that matched independent contractors with customers, and for a time the description held. Drivers, couriers and cleaners valued the ability to refuse work without asking permission, and many combined several applications in a single week to fill the hours they wanted.\n\nThe freedom, however, is unevenly distributed. Platforms set the price, the route, the rating threshold below which a worker is deactivated, and the penalty for declining too many offers. A courier who turns down three jobs in an hour may find the next hour empty. None of this is issued as an instruction, which is precisely the point: the constraints arrive as algorithms rather than as a manager, and the worker experiences them as market conditions rather than as orders. The result is a relationship that looks like self-employment and behaves like employment.\n\nWhat such workers lack is not work but the protections attached to it. Sick pay, holiday pay, accident insurance and the right to appeal a dismissal are, in most systems, triggered by the status of employee. A rider injured in traffic may discover that the platform's insurance covers the customer's parcel and not the rider's leg. Savings are supposed to fill the gap, yet earnings fluctuate from week to week, and the people most dependent on the work are least able to absorb a month without it.\n\nRegulators have responded in three broad ways. Some have reclassified workers as employees, which brings protection but also rigidity: platforms then limit hours, and the flexibility that attracted workers in the first place narrows. Others have created an intermediate category with partial rights, a compromise that has proved administratively messy, since the same person may qualify one month and not the next. A third approach leaves the status alone and requires platforms to contribute to a portable fund, so that benefits travel with the worker from one application to another.\n\nThe debate is often framed as a choice between flexibility and security, as though the two were fixed quantities on a seesaw. In practice the trade-off is a design decision, not a law of nature. A system can offer predictable earnings, injury cover and a genuine choice of hours, provided somebody is willing to pay for them; the question of who that somebody should be is political rather than technical.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"What initially attracted workers to platform work?","options":{"A":"The prospect of a permanent contract with a single firm.","B":"The freedom to decide when and whether to work.","C":"Hourly rates higher than those in conventional jobs.","D":"Training and equipment supplied by the platforms."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段第 4 句说司机、快递员和清洁工看重不必请假就能拒绝工作的能力，第 5 句说不少人同时用几个应用来凑够想做的工时，即自主决定何时做、做不做。A 项长期合同恰是平台模式所没有的；C 项时薪更高原文未提。"},{"n":52,"stem":"The remark that constraints 'arrive as algorithms' suggests that platform control","options":{"A":"is weaker than the control a manager exercises.","B":"operates without being acknowledged as a direct order.","C":"is applied only to newly recruited workers.","D":"is designed jointly by workers and platforms."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段说这些限制不是以指令的形式下达的，工人把它们体验为市场状况而非命令，可见控制并未消失，只是不再以管理者发号施令的面目出现，因而更难被识别和质疑。A 项与控制更弱相矛盾，平台仍设定价格、路线与评分门槛；D 项共同设计一说无据。"},{"n":53,"stem":"The example of the injured rider is used to show that","options":{"A":"platform insurance protects the goods rather than the person doing the work.","B":"riders are usually held responsible for accidents they cause.","C":"customers seldom report damage to their parcels.","D":"accident rates are higher than platforms admit."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 3 段第 3 句说在交通事故中受伤的骑手可能发现，平台的保险保的是顾客的包裹，而不是自己的腿，用来说明保障对象错位。B 项骑手自负其责、D 项事故率被隐瞒，原文均未涉及。"},{"n":54,"stem":"What drawback does the author associate with reclassifying workers as employees?","options":{"A":"It raises the insurance costs paid by customers.","B":"It narrows the flexibility that workers valued.","C":"It excludes part-time workers from all protection.","D":"It obliges platforms to publish their algorithms."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 4 段第 2 句说重新归类为雇员虽带来保障，却也带来刚性：平台随即限制工时，当初吸引工人的灵活性因此收窄。A 项谈顾客保费、D 项谈公开算法，都不是该段内容；C 项把部分保障说成全部排除，属绝对化。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the author's main point in the last paragraph?","options":{"A":"Flexibility and security can never be combined in practice.","B":"Platforms ought to be owned collectively by their workers.","C":"The balance between them is a matter of policy design, not an inevitable trade-off.","D":"Regulation has made gig work less appealing than office work."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。末段第 2 句明确说这种取舍是设计决定而非自然规律，第 3 句说只要有人愿意付钱，可预测的收入、工伤保障与真正的工时选择可以并存，只是由谁付属于政治问题而非技术问题。A 项正是作者要反驳的看法；B 项工人集体所有属额外主张。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on whether community service should be a graduation requirement. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.","prompt_cn":"假设你校正在考虑把社区服务设为毕业要求。请写一篇短文表达你的看法。","word_limit":150,"sample_essay":"Universities exist to prepare students for more than a career, and few experiences teach civic responsibility as directly as service to a community. I therefore support making community service a graduation requirement, provided that the requirement is designed with care.\n\nA mandatory programme ensures that every student, not only those already inclined to volunteer, meets people whose lives differ from their own. A student who spends a term assisting at a neighbourhood legal clinic, or teaching reading to newly arrived children, learns to listen before advising, and begins to see how much of her own success rested on circumstances she did not choose. Lessons of that kind are hard to deliver in a lecture hall.\n\nThe obvious objection is that compulsion breeds resentment and produces hours logged rather than work done. The risk is real but manageable. If students may choose among a range of placements, if the hours required are modest, and if reflection is assessed rather than attendance alone, most will find the work worth doing. A requirement, in short, is not the enemy of commitment; indifference is.","sample_essay_cn":"大学的存在不只是为了让学生谋一份职业，而很少有哪种经历能像服务社区那样直接地教会人公民责任。因此我支持把社区服务设为毕业要求，前提是这一要求经过审慎设计。\n\n强制性的安排能保证每个学生都接触到与自己生活不同的人，而不只是那些本来就愿意做志愿者的人。一个在社区法律咨询点帮忙一学期、或教新来的孩子读书的学生，会先学会倾听再给出建议，也会开始意识到自己的许多顺利并非全凭个人选择。这样的课在阶梯教室里很难上到。\n\n最明显的反对意见是，强制会滋生抵触，只换来一堆打卡时长而非真正的工作。这种风险确实存在，但可以控制。如果学生能在多种岗位中挑选，如果要求的时长适度，如果考核的是反思而不只是出勤，多数人会认为这段经历值得。简言之，要求并不是投入的敌人，冷漠才是。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"乡村振兴战略实施以来，中国农村的面貌发生了明显变化。过去，许多年轻人离村进城打工，留下老人和孩子守着空房。如今，公路和网络延伸到偏远地区，一些年轻人开始返乡创业。他们借助电商平台销售本地的水果、茶叶和手工艺品，把山里的产品卖到千里之外。村里的卫生室、学校和养老服务站也在改善，垃圾和污水得到集中处理。乡村振兴不是把城市模式搬到农村，而是让农民在自己的土地上过上体面的生活，并愿意留下来。","reference_en":"Since the rural revitalisation strategy was introduced, the face of the Chinese countryside has changed noticeably. In the past, many young people left their villages to work in cities, leaving elderly parents and children behind in empty houses. Today, as roads and internet connections reach remote areas, some of the young have begun to return and start businesses. They sell local fruit, tea and handicrafts through e-commerce platforms, sending produce from the mountains to dinner tables a thousand miles away. Village clinics, schools and care centres for the elderly have also improved, and household waste and sewage are now collected and treated. Rural revitalisation does not mean transplanting the urban model into the countryside; it means enabling farmers to live decent lives on their own land, and to want to stay.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet6-mock-3":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":3,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第3套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"Human bodies keep time with an internal clock that expects darkness at night and light by day. When work schedules ignore that expectation, the effects reach far beyond [[26]] fatigue at the end of a shift. Bright factory lighting, arriving at the hour when the body expects dusk, tends to [[27]] the production of melatonin, the hormone that prepares us for sleep. Body temperature and blood pressure, which ought to fall during the night, remain [[28]] for hours. Meanwhile the digestive system, braced for rest, must process a heavy meal at three in the morning.\n\nEconomists put the [[29]] at hundreds of billions a year in lost output and medical bills. Large surveys of nurses and transport staff have also linked years of rotating shifts to a higher [[30]] of metabolic disorder, weight gain and heart disease. One explanation is that the organs drift out of step with one another: the liver may be working on one timetable while the brain is still on another, so the hormones that [[31]] appetite and blood sugar no longer arrive at the moment the body can use them.\n\nSome employers have tried to [[32]] the damage through better design. Forward-rotating schedules, in which a worker moves from morning to afternoon to night rather than the reverse, suit the clock's natural [[33]] to run slightly long. They also acknowledge that the body does not switch in a single night: it adapts [[34]], by roughly an hour a day. None of these measures makes night work harmless, but together they [[35]] the strain.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"adjust"},{"k":"B","t":"conceal"},{"k":"C","t":"abruptly"},{"k":"D","t":"modest"},{"k":"E","t":"ease"},{"k":"F","t":"erratic"},{"k":"G","t":"shortage"},{"k":"H","t":"incidence"},{"k":"I","t":"offset"},{"k":"J","t":"regulate"},{"k":"K","t":"mere"},{"k":"L","t":"suppress"},{"k":"M","t":"tendency"},{"k":"N","t":"gradually"},{"k":"O","t":"cost"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"K","explanation":"答案 K) mere。空处修饰 fatigue，与前面的 far beyond 构成「远不止」的递进，mere 意为「仅仅的」，与 at the end of a shift 这种轻描淡写的说法相呼应。D modest 只表示程度温和，撑不起「不止于此」的让步语气；F erratic 修饰疲劳不搭配。"},{"n":27,"answer":"L","explanation":"答案 L) suppress。第 1 段第 2 句说工厂强光恰好出现在身体期待黄昏的时刻，tends to 后接动词原形，而褪黑素本是 prepares us for sleep 的激素，强光自然「抑制」其分泌。J regulate 看似合理，但上下文强调节律被打乱而非被调节；B conceal 不能与激素分泌搭配。"},{"n":28,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) erratic。remain 后接形容词作表语，前面的 which ought to fall during the night 说明本应平稳下降却「忽高忽低」，erratic 正合此意。D modest 指幅度温和，与 for hours 暗示的失控相反；K mere 不能作表语描述生理指标。"},{"n":29,"answer":"O","explanation":"答案 O) cost。put the cost at ... 是固定说法，后接 hundreds of billions a year in lost output and medical bills，金额线索明确指向经济代价。G shortage 无法与金额搭配；M tendency 不能用于 put ... at 结构。"},{"n":30,"answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H) incidence。a higher ___ of metabolic disorder 需要表示「发生率」的名词，incidence of 为固定搭配。M tendency 后应接 to do 而非 of，语法上即被排除；O cost 与 disorder 连用会变成「疾病的花费」，与 linked ... to 的因果表述不符。"},{"n":31,"answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J) regulate。定语从句先行词是 hormones，空格后接 appetite and blood sugar 两个并列宾语，只有 regulate 能同时与二者搭配，意为「调节」。L suppress 只能带 appetite，与 blood sugar 连用不合逻辑；A adjust 不能直接以 appetite 作宾语。"},{"n":32,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) offset。try to 后接动词原形，through better design 说明是以改进排班来「抵消」损害。B conceal 语法可行，但下文列举的全是减轻伤害的措施，与「隐瞒」相反；E ease 通常搭配 strain、pain，不搭配 damage。"},{"n":33,"answer":"M","explanation":"答案 M) tendency。the clock's natural ___ to run slightly long 中，to run 是判定依据：能接不定式且表示「倾向」的名词只有 tendency。H incidence 后常接 of；O cost 与 to run long 无法构成语义。"},{"n":34,"answer":"N","explanation":"答案 N) gradually。冒号后的 by roughly an hour a day 说明适应是「逐步」发生的，与前半句 does not switch in a single night 完全一致。C abruptly 与之恰好相反；D modest 是形容词，不能修饰 adapts。"},{"n":35,"answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E) ease。but together they 引出转折后的正面作用，空格后接 the strain，ease the strain 意为「减轻负担」。I offset 已用于第 32 题，且多接 damage、cost；A adjust 不能以 strain 为宾语。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For three decades the standing of a university department in much of the world has been read off a single number: how many papers its staff place in journals that somebody else has ranked. The habit is easy to explain and hard to break. A count can be produced by an administrator in an afternoon, compared across institutions, and defended in a budget meeting. What it cannot do is say whether the work counted was any good, or whether the person who produced it will still be asking interesting questions in ten years. That gap is now the subject of an unusually wide reform."},{"label":"B","text":"The fashion for counting did not begin with malice. In the 1980s and 1990s, as public funding tightened, ministries demanded evidence that money was being well spent, and universities needed a language that auditors understood. Citation indices, journal rankings and grant totals were already sitting in databases, so they became that language. Each was a rough proxy for quality, and each was soon treated as though it were quality itself. Once appointments and promotions were tied to the proxies, staff had every reason to optimise for them, and the proxies quietly became the purpose they had been invented to measure."},{"label":"C","text":"The behavioural effects were predictable and are now well documented. Researchers split findings into the smallest publishable units, because four short papers count more than one long one. They avoid questions whose answers might take a decade, because the review cycle rewards visible annual output. Young scholars feel this most sharply, since they need a contract renewed long before they can afford to be patient; several surveys report that early-career staff are the least willing to change field or take a speculative risk. The result is a literature that grows quickly and, in some fields, advances slowly."},{"label":"D","text":"The most widely discussed remedy is the representative works system. Instead of listing everything an applicant has published, a candidate submits three to five pieces chosen as the best evidence of what he or she can do, together with a short statement explaining why each was selected and what it contributed. Evaluators read the pieces themselves rather than their titles. Advocates argue that the format puts judgement back at the centre of assessment and removes the incentive to pad a list. Critics warn that it hands enormous discretion to panels and may simply relocate the old favouritism."},{"label":"E","text":"Peer review is the mechanism on which the whole design depends, and it is also its weakest joint. Reviewers are busy, unpaid and often drawn from the same small circle of networks that produced the candidate. Studies of grant panels have found that scores shift when the same proposals are read in a different order, and that the identity of the chair accounts for a surprising share of the variance. None of this makes review worthless; it makes it something that has to be designed. Randomisation, written rather than spoken scoring, and readers from outside the field are among the fixes now being tested."},{"label":"F","text":"A second complaint concerns teaching. In institutions that reward only publication, lecturing becomes a duty to be discharged with as little interference as possible, and students notice. Reform proposals therefore usually include a teaching portfolio: course designs, samples of feedback, evidence of what students actually learned. The difficulty is measurement. Research output, however imperfectly, leaves a trail; good teaching often leaves only a memory. Departments that have tried portfolios report that the documents are hard to compare and easy to write well without teaching well, which is why some have turned to structured observation of classes instead."},{"label":"G","text":"Any single template runs into the fact that disciplines do not share a tempo. A mathematician may produce two papers in a decade and change the subject; a clinical team may produce forty, each reporting a trial that could not have been run in any other way. Historians publish books, economists publish articles, and engineers publish patents that no journal would accept. A national framework that ignores these differences will either crush the slow disciplines or inflate the fast ones. The Corwin Commission, which spent four years on the question, concluded that the unit of assessment should be the department's own account of what it is trying to do."},{"label":"H","text":"Pressure also arrives from outside the sector. International league tables weigh research income and citation counts heavily, and governments that fund universities by results tend to import the same weights. A ministry that wants its flag higher in the table has an obvious interest in more papers, not better ones. Reformers therefore face an awkward task: they must persuade the same officials who commissioned the counting to accept a slower and less legible kind of evidence. Where that persuasion has succeeded, it has usually come with a protected budget, so that departments are not forced to choose between reputation and survival."},{"label":"I","text":"No evaluation system is neutral, and every one of them will be gamed. The realistic aim is not to abolish gaming but to make the game worth playing. A department that shifts from counting papers to reading them will find that its staff spend more time on fewer, better projects, and it will also find that colleagues who were efficient at producing short papers become less comfortable. That discomfort is a cost, and honest reformers should say so plainly rather than promise that nothing will be lost along the way."},{"label":"J","text":"What the reform ultimately asks is a question universities have always found awkward: what is scholarship for? If the answer is a contribution to understanding, then the evidence must include the judgement of people who understand the work, and it must allow for the possibility that the most valuable contribution looks unimpressive for years. Metrics can support that judgement; they cannot replace it. The institutions that get this right will not be the ones with the most elaborate formulas, but the ones whose committees are trusted enough to be given real discretion."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"The use of counts spread when funders began demanding proof that public money was being well spent.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B 段。该段交代历史成因：上世纪八九十年代公共经费收紧，主管部门要求证明资金用得其所，而引用指数与期刊排名「本来就在数据库里」，于是成了审计者听得懂的语言，与题干 funders demanding proof 对应。A 段只讲计数当下的方便，没有交代它为何兴起。"},{"n":37,"stem":"Breaking results into several short papers is a rational response to the way output is measured.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C 段。该段说研究者把发现拆成 the smallest publishable units，理由是四篇短文比一篇长文更「算数」，正是题干 rational response 的改写。D 段谈代表作制度如何提交材料，与拆分论文无关。"},{"n":38,"stem":"Under the proposed format, assessors are expected to engage with the substance of a few selected pieces rather than tally titles.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D 段。该段说明候选人提交三到五篇自选作品，评审「读作品本身而不是标题」，对应题干 engage with the substance 与 rather than tally titles。E 段讨论的是评审环节的缺陷，而非制度对评审者的要求。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Even expert judgement can be swayed by features of the process that have nothing to do with quality.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E 段。该段列举同一批申请换个顺序阅读分数就会变、主席身份能解释相当一部分方差，说明判断受与质量无关的因素左右，与题干 swayed by irrelevant features 对应。F 段谈教学档案难比较，不涉及评审偏差。"},{"n":40,"stem":"Good teaching leaves few durable records, which complicates any attempt to reward it.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F 段。该段说研究成果总会留下痕迹，而好教学往往只留下记忆，因此教学档案 hard to compare，对应题干 few durable records 与 complicates。G 段讨论学科节奏差异，与教学记录无关。"},{"n":41,"stem":"Publication habits vary so much between fields that one uniform standard is likely to distort some of them.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G 段。该段以数学、临床团队、历史学、工程学为例说明产出节奏不同，并指出统一框架会 either crush the slow disciplines or inflate the fast ones，与题干 distort 对应。H 段讲的是外部排名的压力，不是学科差异。"},{"n":42,"stem":"External league tables push institutions towards exactly the behaviour that reformers wish to discourage.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H 段。该段说国际排名看重科研经费与引用数，按绩效拨款的政府会把同样的权重搬过来，于是主管部门更想要 more papers, not better ones，对应题干 push institutions towards。G 段未提及排名或外部压力。"},{"n":43,"stem":"Reformers should admit that a better system will still impose losses on some members of staff.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I 段。该段承认改革会让擅长写短文的人变得不那么自在，并说 That discomfort is a cost，诚实的改革者应当讲清楚，对应题干 admit 与 impose losses。J 段是收束性的总结，没有谈到具体代价。"},{"n":44,"stem":"The worth of a piece of research may become apparent only long after it appears.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J 段。该段说最有价值的贡献可能 looks unimpressive for years，因此评价必须为此留出空间，与题干 only long after it appears 对应。D 段只讲提交与阅读方式，不涉及时间维度。"},{"n":45,"stem":"The counting habit survives partly because it is convenient for the people who run institutions.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A 段。该段说一个数字行政人员一个下午就能算出来，还能跨校比较、在预算会上为自己辩护，正是题干 convenient for administrators 的展开。B 段讲的是历史成因，不是当下的便利。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Walk into any design-conscious apartment and you meet the aesthetic of subtraction: bare walls, one good chair, a shelf holding four books and a stone. Advertisers call this minimalism, and they sell it with the same urgency that once sold clutter. A plain linen cushion costs more than an ornate one, because restraint, it turns out, requires expensive materials and considerable confidence. Critics note the irony: a movement that began as a protest against acquisition has become a reliable way of persuading people to acquire.\n\nThe original argument was not about taste. Its target was the machinery of wanting: the assumption, rarely examined, that a life improves in proportion to the number of things in it. Writers in this tradition observed that possessions demand attention, since they must be cleaned, insured, stored and worried about, and that attention is the one resource nobody can manufacture. A smaller number of better things was offered as a way of spending less of oneself on maintenance. That claim concerns time and attention rather than beauty, and survives whether or not the objects are attractive.\n\nSomething was lost in translation. When the argument reached the market it was recast as a look rather than a practice, and a look can be purchased. The result is a curious loop: the buyer who wishes to signal detachment from consumerism buys a carefully curated set of objects to signal it with. Sociologists have a name for goods valued chiefly for what they say about their owner, and the pale, empty room says a great deal. It says that the owner can afford space and need not keep things for want of replacements.\n\nThere is a harder version that resists this fate. It begins with an inventory rather than a purchase, and often produces less attractive rooms. Its questions are unglamorous: which of these objects do I use, and what would happen if I did not own it? People who work through such questions frequently discover that the most expensive item in the house is not the one they regret buying but the one they are paying to store. The savings, in money and in attention, are real; they are simply invisible in photographs.\n\nNone of this means the aesthetic is dishonest, only that it is not the argument. A person may keep a beautiful, sparse room and think clearly about consumption; another may keep a crowded one and think equally clearly. What separates them is not the number of objects but whether the objects were chosen or merely accumulated. Judged by that standard, minimalism as a style is a distraction, and as a discipline it is a question most households would benefit from asking once a decade.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What does the author suggest about plain, restrained objects in the first paragraph?","options":{"A":"They are chosen mainly by people who cannot afford ornament.","B":"They are produced in smaller quantities than decorated goods.","C":"They often cost more than elaborate ones because simplicity is expensive to achieve.","D":"They appeal chiefly to buyers who dislike modern design."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 1 段第 3 句说 A plain linen cushion costs more than an ornate one, because restraint ... requires expensive materials，即「简洁」本身要花更多钱。A 项说买主买不起装饰，与 expensive materials 直接相反；B 项谈产量，原文根本没有提及。"},{"n":47,"stem":"According to the second paragraph, the original case for owning fewer things rested on","options":{"A":"the belief that beautiful objects improve daily life.","B":"the wish to imitate the habits of wealthier households.","C":"the fear that clutter does permanent damage to health.","D":"the observation that possessions consume time and attention."},"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D。第 2 段第 3 句指出 possessions demand attention ... and attention is the one resource nobody can manufacture，少拥有是为了少耗费时间与注意力。A 项把审美当成立论依据，而该段明说 The original argument was not about taste；C 项的健康损害属于无据添加。"},{"n":48,"stem":"What does the author refer to by saying that the idea resists 'this fate' (Para. 4)?","options":{"A":"Being dismissed by critics as insincere.","B":"Being turned into a style that can be bought.","C":"Being abandoned by its original supporters.","D":"Being confused with ordinary household thrift."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。this fate 回指上一段：极简主义进入市场后被改写成 a look rather than a practice，而 a look can be purchased，即被变成可以买到的风格。A 项「被批评者否定」并非第三段所述；D 项「与节俭混淆」原文没有这一层。"},{"n":49,"stem":"What do people commonly discover when they examine what they own, according to the fourth paragraph?","options":{"A":"They are paying more to keep things than the things are worth.","B":"They have spent too much on objects they rarely use.","C":"They own fewer items than they had previously assumed.","D":"They would rather replace old objects than repair them."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 4 段倒数第二句说，最贵的不是后悔买的那件，而是 the one they are paying to store，即持续为存放付费，对应「保管成本超过物品价值」。B 项「买了不用」是常识性推测，原文的落点在储存开销；C 项与做清单的语境相反。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What is the author's view of minimalism as a style?","options":{"A":"It is a harmless fashion that deserves no criticism at all.","B":"It conveys the original argument more clearly than words can.","C":"It addresses appearance rather than the underlying practice.","D":"It should be replaced by stricter rules about ownership."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。末段说 What separates them is not the number of objects but whether the objects were chosen or merely accumulated，并把作为风格的极简主义称作 a distraction，即它处理的是外观而非选择本身。A 项「无害」与 distraction 的贬抑语气不符；D 项主张更严格的规则，而原文主张的是「是否经过选择」。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"The ocean receives roughly eleven million tonnes of plastic a year, a figure repeated so often that it has lost the power to shock. What is less often said is that the material arrives by a small number of routes. Packaging floats down rivers; fishing gear is cut loose in storms; fibres escape washing machines and tyre treads. A policy aimed at the gyres themselves, the great rotating patches where debris accumulates, would be aimed at the symptom, since the patches are where plastic ends up, not where it starts.\n\nRecycling is the remedy most often proposed and the least able to deliver on its own. Only a small fraction of the plastic ever produced has been recycled, and much of what is collected is not recycled but downcycled: a bottle becomes a park bench, and the bench becomes landfill. The technical reason is that most packaging is a laminate of several materials bonded together, and separating them costs more than the recovered plastic is worth. The economic reason is that virgin plastic, made from cheap fossil fuel, usually undercuts the recycled kind.\n\nDesign offers a quieter route. A bottle made of a single polymer can be reprocessed repeatedly, whereas the same bottle with a glued label and a multilayer cap cannot. Several large brands have therefore begun to simplify packaging, not out of sentiment but because collection schemes in parts of Europe now charge producers by weight and by difficulty. Refill systems, which return the container to the seller, go further, removing the package from the waste stream altogether. Both approaches share a feature that recycling lacks: they change what is made rather than what is done with it afterwards.\n\nThe diplomatic track has moved more slowly and matters more. A proposed international agreement would, in its current draft, oblige signatories to set binding limits on production rather than merely on leakage, and to publish the chemicals used in plastics. Industry representatives argue that caps on production punish manufacturers for a problem created by poor waste management. Public-health groups reply that additives are precisely the part of the problem that recycling cannot touch, since a toxic additive survives every mechanical process.\n\nThe temptation in all of this is to look for a single lever. There is none: an agreement without collection infrastructure produces signatures and little else, and collection without redesigned packaging produces bales that nobody will buy. What the evidence does support is a rough ordering. The cheapest gains are upstream, in what gets made and how much of it; the most expensive are downstream, in the machines that fish it out again. Policies that reverse that order tend to be popular and ineffective.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"Why does the author regard a policy aimed at the ocean gyres as inadequate?","options":{"A":"The patches lie too far from land for ships to reach economically.","B":"They are places where plastic gathers rather than where it originates.","C":"Most of the material there has already broken into tiny particles.","D":"International law does not cover waters beyond national boundaries."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段末句说 the patches are where plastic ends up, not where it starts，即环流区是塑料汇聚之地而非源头，故针对此处治理只触及症状。A 项「船开不到」原文未提；C 项「已碎成微粒」属过度推断，文中没有这一步。"},{"n":52,"stem":"What makes much of today's packaging difficult to recycle?","options":{"A":"It contains materials that cannot be melted down at all.","B":"Consumers seldom remove labels before throwing containers away.","C":"Its layers are bonded together and expensive to separate.","D":"Collection schemes refuse to accept containers that held food."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 2 段第 3 句说多数包装是 a laminate of several materials bonded together，而 separating them costs more than the recovered plastic is worth，即分层粘合、分离成本过高。A 项「完全不能熔融」说得过死；D 项「拒收食品容器」原文没有依据。"},{"n":53,"stem":"The word 'undercuts' (Para. 2) is closest in meaning to","options":{"A":"is produced more quickly than","B":"is sold at a lower price than","C":"is regulated less strictly than","D":"is advertised more widely than"},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。该词所在句说 virgin plastic, made from cheap fossil fuel, usually undercuts the recycled kind，cheap 与「价格更低」构成因果，故 undercuts 意为「比……卖得便宜」。A、C、D 分别指向速度、监管与宣传，都与 cheap fossil fuel 这一线索无关。"},{"n":54,"stem":"What do simplified packaging and refill systems have in common?","options":{"A":"Both depend on consumers sorting their waste correctly.","B":"Both have so far been adopted mainly by small companies.","C":"Both raise the price paid by the ordinary consumer.","D":"Both alter what is produced instead of what happens to it later."},"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D。第 3 段末句说两者 share a feature that recycling lacks: they change what is made rather than what is done with it afterwards，与 D 项同义。A 项「依赖消费者分类」恰是回收的特征；B 项与原文 Several large brands 相反。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What does the author conclude about policies for dealing with plastic waste?","options":{"A":"A binding international agreement would solve most of the problem.","B":"Recycling should be abandoned in favour of refill schemes.","C":"The most effective measures act before waste is created.","D":"Downstream technologies deserve far more funding than they receive."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。末段说 The cheapest gains are upstream, in what gets made and how much of it，并指出颠倒这一次序的政策 popular and ineffective，即最有效的措施发生在废物产生之前。A 项与 an agreement without collection infrastructure produces signatures and little else 相反；B 项「放弃回收」属过度推断。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on whether technology brings people closer together or drives them apart. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.","prompt_cn":"有人认为科技让人与人更加亲近，也有人认为科技使人彼此疏远。请就此写一篇短文，说明你的看法。","word_limit":150,"sample_essay":"The argument that technology isolates us has a certain appeal, and a certain laziness. It is true that a family may sit at one table, each member attending to a different screen, and that a message sent from the next room is not the same as a conversation. Yet the comparison is usually made against an imagined past in which everyone lived within walking distance of the people they loved.\n\nFor those who have moved away, the change is not subtle. A grandparent can watch a child grow up across an ocean; a student can keep a friendship alive through years of separation. Distance used to end relationships quietly, simply because maintaining them cost more effort than most people could spare.\n\nThe honest conclusion is that technology amplifies whatever intention we bring to it. It offers new ways of avoiding people and new ways of reaching them, and it does not choose between the two. Whether it draws us together depends less on the devices than on the habits we build around them.","sample_essay_cn":"认为科技使人孤立的说法既有一分道理，也带着一分懒惰。一家人坐在同一张桌边、各自盯着一块屏幕，这确实是事实；从隔壁房间发一条消息，也确实不等同于一次交谈。然而这种比较通常是以一个想象中的过去为参照的——在那个过去里，人人所爱之人都住在步行可达的地方。\n\n对于离乡在外的人来说，变化并不细微。祖父母可以隔着大洋看着孙辈长大；一个学生可以在多年分离中维持一段友谊。过去，距离会悄无声息地终结一段关系，仅仅因为维系它所需的努力超出了多数人所能负担的范围。\n\n诚实的结论是：科技放大了我们带给它的意图。它既提供了回避他人的新方式，也提供了抵达他人的新方式，而它本身并不在两者之间做出选择。它究竟让我们更亲近还是更疏远，取决于我们围绕这些设备养成的习惯，而不是设备本身。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"二十年来，中国保护非物质文化遗产的方式变化明显。早期重点是记录技艺：拍摄影像，整理成书，以免人亡艺绝。如今人们更强调让传统在日常生活中继续生长：一些地方把老手艺引入课程，一些村落把节庆恢复为社区活动，而非供游客围观的表演。这种转变并不轻松，活态传承既需要传承人有稳定收入，也需要年轻人愿意长期学艺。保护的目的不是把文化制成标本，而是给它留下继续变化的余地。","reference_en":"Over the past two decades, the way China protects its intangible cultural heritage has changed markedly. Early efforts concentrated on recording skills: filming them and compiling them into books, so that a craft would not disappear with its last practitioner. Today the emphasis has shifted towards letting traditions keep growing in everyday life. Some places have brought old crafts into school curricula, and some villages have restored festivals as events belonging to the community itself rather than as performances staged for tourists to watch. This shift has not been easy, because living transmission requires both a steady income for inheritors and young people willing to spend years learning a craft. The purpose of protection is not to turn culture into a specimen, but to leave it room to keep changing.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}},"cet6-mock-4":{"schema":"cet-bank/1","meta":{"level":"cet6","year":2026,"month":1,"set":4,"title":"原创模拟 六级 第4套","source":"原创模拟题（本项目作者撰写，非真题）","mock":true},"reading":[{"key":"A","type":"banked_cloze","name":"Section A 选词填空","short":"选词填空","instructions":"从 15 个单词中为每个空选一个，每词只用一次。","passage":"For half a century, transport planning treated the street as a pipe for moving vehicles and measured success in seconds saved per kilometre. The result in many cities was a network of wide, fast roads that left a deep [[26]] between neighbourhoods. A newer school of urban design begins from a rather different [[27]]: a street is a room, and the people in it are hosts and guests rather than traffic. Three ideas [[28]] the argument.\nThe first is scale. A walkable street is not merely a short one; it is one whose width, kerb radius and signage [[29]] the speed a driver feels safe adopting. Very narrow lanes and tight corners [[30]] drivers to slow down without a single sign, because the road itself offers no invitation to hurry.\nThe second is connectivity. A grid of small blocks multiplies the routes available, so a ten-minute walk may [[31]] a dozen alternatives. Cul-de-sacs, by contrast, concentrate traffic on a few arteries and make every trip [[32]] on the same crowded road. Connectivity also spreads custom: a shop on a well-connected street draws from a wider [[33]] than one stranded at the end of a dead end.\nThe third is the street-facing interface. Blank walls and car parks at ground level [[34]] the very activity that makes walking pleasant. Doors, windows and stalls close to the pavement provide what designers call eyes on the street, a form of supervision that is exercised [[35]] but to remarkable effect.","word_options":[{"k":"A","t":"sustain"},{"k":"B","t":"catchment"},{"k":"C","t":"rigid"},{"k":"D","t":"underpin"},{"k":"E","t":"threshold"},{"k":"F","t":"prompt"},{"k":"G","t":"modest"},{"k":"H","t":"rift"},{"k":"I","t":"govern"},{"k":"J","t":"scarcely"},{"k":"K","t":"dependent"},{"k":"L","t":"premise"},{"k":"M","t":"suppress"},{"k":"N","t":"unobtrusively"},{"k":"O","t":"yield"}],"questions":[{"n":26,"answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H) rift。第一段第二句说这些宽而快的道路 left a deep ___ between neighbourhoods，rift 意为『裂痕』，与 between 搭配表示道路把社区割开。E) threshold 指门槛，不能作 left 的宾语表达分隔；G) modest 是形容词，此处需要名词。"},{"n":27,"answer":"L","explanation":"答案 L) premise。第一段第三句 begins from a rather different ___，冒号后的 a street is a room 正是这一前提的内容，premise 意为『前提』。A) sustain 是动词，无法出现在 a different 之后作名词。"},{"n":28,"answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D) underpin。第一段末句 Three ideas ___ the argument，主语为复数、句子为一般现在时，underpin 意为『支撑』，与 ideas 和 argument 的搭配自然。F) prompt 须接某人做某事，不能直接带 the argument。"},{"n":29,"answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I) govern。第二段第二句主语是 width, kerb radius and signage，空格后为 the speed a driver feels safe adopting，govern 表示『决定、左右』，与 speed 搭配。M) suppress 意为『压制』，与街道尺度决定车速的逻辑相反。"},{"n":30,"answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F) prompt。第二段第三句 Narrow lanes and tight corners ___ drivers to slow down，prompt sb to do sth 为固定搭配，意为『促使』。I) govern 不能接 to do；D) underpin 与 drivers 搭配不通。"},{"n":31,"answer":"O","explanation":"答案 O) yield。第三段第二句 so a ten-minute walk may ___ a dozen alternatives，yield 意为『产生、带来』，与 alternatives 构成动宾关系。A) sustain 意为『维持』，无法表达『提供多种选择』之意。"},{"n":32,"answer":"K","explanation":"答案 K) dependent。第三段第三句 make every trip ___ on the same crowded road，此处为 make + 宾语 + 形容词结构，dependent on 是固定搭配。C) rigid 不能与 on 连用表示依赖。"},{"n":33,"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B) catchment。第三段末句 draws from a wider ___ than one stranded at the end of a dead end，catchment 指商店的『客源范围』，与 draws from、wider 呼应。E) threshold 指阈值或门槛，不能表示客源范围。"},{"n":34,"answer":"M","explanation":"答案 M) suppress。第四段第二句 Blank walls and car parks at ground level ___ the very activity，suppress 意为『压制』，与 the very activity 构成合理动宾关系。A) sustain 意为『维持』，与空白墙面带来的负面作用相反。"},{"n":35,"answer":"N","explanation":"答案 N) unobtrusively。第四段末句 a form of supervision that is exercised ___ but to remarkable effect，需副词修饰 exercised，unobtrusively 意为『不引人注意地』，与后面的 remarkable effect 形成让步对比。J) scarcely 意为『几乎不』，与 remarkable effect 自相矛盾。"}]},{"key":"B","type":"paragraph_matching","name":"Section B 段落匹配","short":"段落匹配","instructions":"为每句话找到对应的段落字母，段落可重复使用。","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"The notion that education is something completed in youth and then used for forty years is a historical anomaly rather than a natural law. For most of the industrial era a single trade could sustain a working life, and a person who changed occupation twice was considered unusual. Today the useful half-life of a technical skill is often reckoned in years rather than decades. Institutions, however, still behave as though the sequence were fixed: study, then work, then retire. The mismatch is not primarily a shortage of courses. It is a shortage of permission, money and time to return to study once a person has become expensive to employ."},{"label":"B","text":"Micro-credentials were meant to solve exactly that problem. A short course, assessed and badged, can be completed in weeks and stacked with others into something resembling a qualification. On paper the logic is elegant: learners pay less, employers receive evidence of a specific skill, and universities reach people who would never enrol in a full degree. In practice the market has fragmented. Hundreds of providers issue badges under incompatible rules, and a certificate that takes three weeks to earn struggles to tell an employer much that a line on a CV could not. The currency has inflated faster than its value has grown."},{"label":"C","text":"Employers have responded by building academies of their own. A retail group with sixty thousand staff may run induction, compliance and leadership programmes in-house, and some now award credentials that carry weight inside the company. The advantages are real: the curriculum matches the work, and study fits around shifts rather than competing with them. The risk is narrower. A qualification designed around one firm's systems may lose much of its value the moment the employee leaves, which discourages precisely the mobility that a changing economy requires. Learning that cannot travel is training, not education."},{"label":"D","text":"Online provision promised to dissolve the geography of opportunity, and in one respect it has: a student in a small town can now follow a lecture given by a specialist on another continent. What it has not solved is persistence. Enrolment is cheap and therefore casual, and completion rates in open online courses have long hovered in the single digits. Platforms have responded with shorter units, reminders and paid certificates, yet the underlying difficulty remains social rather than technical. People finish what connects them to other people, and a screen alone provides very little of that connection."},{"label":"E","text":"The hardest transitions happen in mid-life, when the cost of returning to study is highest. A forty-five-year-old with a mortgage, ageing parents and children in school cannot easily take a year out, and evening classes compete with everything else that is already squeezed. Dr Susan Vick, who studies adult learning at the Marlow Centre, observes that such learners rarely lack motivation; what they lack is permission to be beginners again. Programmes that treat them as deficient tend to lose them within weeks, while those that build on the judgement they already possess recruit far more successfully."},{"label":"F","text":"Even well-designed courses run into the filter problem. Employers say they value skills, then screen applicants by degree classification because it is cheap and legible. A candidate who has completed four excellent short courses may still be invisible to a recruitment system that asks for a bachelor's degree, and the rejection arrives without explanation. Some firms have begun to publish skills-based job descriptions and to test candidates directly, but the practice is uneven. Until hiring routines change, credentials that do not fit the filter will struggle to reward the people who earn them."},{"label":"G","text":"Who pays is the question that decides whether any of this scales. Individuals can be asked to invest in their own futures, and many do, but the burden falls hardest on those with least to invest and most to gain. Several governments have experimented with learning accounts, vouchers and tax relief, with mixed results: take-up is concentrated among people who were already well qualified. Levies on employers have a clearer logic, since the firms that benefit contribute, yet small businesses complain that the administration costs more than the training it funds."},{"label":"H","text":"There is also a persistent misreading of what older learners bring. A study at the Marlow Centre followed two hundred workers who retrained after the age of forty and found that their advantage was rarely speed of acquisition. It was judgement: knowing which questions to ask, which shortcuts are unsafe, and when a tidy answer is probably wrong. Employers who treat retraining as a repair job miss this entirely. The most productive arrangements pair experienced people with new tools instead of replacing one with the other, and the results tend to appear within a single project cycle."},{"label":"I","text":"Assessment remains the weakest link. A certificate is only as good as the evidence behind it, and evidence is expensive to produce. Recognition of prior learning, which allows people to convert experience into academic credit, sounds generous but often collapses into paperwork that favours those with well-documented careers. Portfolios, simulations and supervised projects offer richer signals, though they resist comparison across providers. The field has not agreed on what a credible claim of competence looks like, and until it does, badges will multiply faster than trust in them. That is a problem for learners and for employers alike."},{"label":"J","text":"The temptation is to turn all of this into a story about individual effort, in which those who keep learning thrive and those who do not are left behind. That framing is convenient for institutions and unkind to people, since it converts a structural question into a personal failing. Learning across a working life depends on arrangements: time that employers release, money that governments or firms supply, and qualifications that travel across borders and sectors. Treat lifelong learning as infrastructure rather than as rescue, and the policy questions become clearer, if not easier."}],"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"Presenting adaptation as a personal responsibility serves organisations while burdening individuals unfairly.","answer":"J","explanation":"答案 J。第 10 段第二句 That framing is convenient for institutions and unkind to people，并把结构性问题转成 personal failing。A 段只指出制度假设已经过时，没有这层批评。"},{"n":37,"stem":"Qualifications built around one employer's working methods may make staff less willing to move to another organisation.","answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 3 段倒数第二句说这类资格一旦离职便贬值，which discourages precisely the mobility that a changing economy requires，即抑制流动。B 段讲微证书市场碎片化，未涉及雇主专属资格。"},{"n":38,"stem":"The idea that schooling belongs only to the early part of life reflects a particular period in history rather than a permanent truth.","answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 1 段首句称这一观念是 a historical anomaly rather than a natural law，与题干『特定历史时期的产物而非永恒真理』对应。E 段讨论中年转学的成本，J 段讨论归因于个人努力，均未涉及观念的历史性。"},{"n":39,"stem":"Hiring systems that sort applicants by degree can prevent skilled candidates from being considered at all.","answer":"F","explanation":"答案 F。第 6 段第三句说完成四门优质短课的求职者 may still be invisible to a recruitment system that asks for a bachelor's degree。G 段谈经费来源，与筛选机制无关。"},{"n":40,"stem":"Research indicates that the principal strength of people who retrain in middle age is not how quickly they pick things up.","answer":"H","explanation":"答案 H。第 8 段第二句 found that their advantage was rarely speed of acquisition，随后说明优势在 judgement。E 段引用同一机构的研究，但讲的是学习动机与课程设计。"},{"n":41,"stem":"The worth of short certificates has been undermined by the absence of agreed standards among the bodies that issue them.","answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段倒数第二句说 Hundreds of providers issue badges under incompatible rules，加上 The currency has inflated，即标准不一导致凭证贬值。C 段谈企业自办学院，与发证机构的标准无关。"},{"n":42,"stem":"Efforts to grant credit for work experience often end up benefiting those whose careers are easiest to document.","answer":"I","explanation":"答案 I。第 9 段第三句说 recognition of prior learning 常沦为 paperwork that favours those with well-documented careers。B 段谈证书标准不一，讨论对象不同。"},{"n":43,"stem":"What prevents people from finishing open online courses is chiefly a matter of human contact rather than of technology.","answer":"D","explanation":"答案 D。第 4 段倒数第二句 the underlying difficulty remains social rather than technical，末句 People finish what connects them to other people 进一步点明。A 段谈的是课程供给不足，与此无关。"},{"n":44,"stem":"Arrangements that require individuals to finance their own retraining mostly attract those who are already well qualified.","answer":"G","explanation":"答案 G。第 7 段第三句说 learning accounts、vouchers 等试验的结果是 take-up is concentrated among people who were already well qualified。E 段谈中年人的时间压力，不是经费分担问题。"},{"n":45,"stem":"Courses for learners in their forties work best when they recognise existing competence instead of assuming a deficit.","answer":"E","explanation":"答案 E。第 5 段末句说把学员当作 deficient 的项目会流失他们，而 build on the judgement they already possess 的项目招生更好。H 段虽也谈年长学员，但侧重其判断力的具体表现，不是课程设计原则。"}]},{"key":"C1","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage One）","short":"仔细阅读一","instructions":"","passage":"Ask people whether they care about privacy and most say yes, emphatically. Observe what they do and the picture blurs. The same person who denounces data collection will accept cookies without reading the notice, hand over a fingerprint to unlock a phone, and let a map application record every journey for the sake of a faster route home. This gap between stated belief and behaviour is usually reported as hypocrisy. It is better understood as a rational response to an unequal exchange.\nThe exchange is unequal because the costs of refusing are immediate and the costs of consenting are deferred. Declining a cookie banner may mean losing access to a page; refusing location data may mean a taxi that cannot find you. The benefit of protection, by contrast, is invisible and statistical: a fraud that does not happen, a profile that is not sold. People are notoriously poor at weighing a certain small inconvenience against an uncertain large harm, and firms have learned to exploit that asymmetry with considerable skill.\nRegulators have tried to correct the imbalance through consent. Require the notice, assume the answer is no until the user says yes, and the market will supposedly deliver the level of sharing people actually want. In practice consent has become a ritual. Notices are long, options are buried, and the design of the interface nudges toward the permissive choice. A right that can only be exercised through a form nobody reads is a right in name only. Consent was meant to be a brake; it has become a formality that legitimises the speed.\nA more promising approach treats some data as off-limits regardless of what anyone agrees to. Certain categories, including health records, precise location and the patterns that reveal who a person's friends are, are difficult to un-share once released, and the individual is poorly placed to judge the risk. Setting floors below which no bargain is permitted does not insult consumers' intelligence; it acknowledges that some transactions are too complex for case-by-case negotiation. The alternative, endless disclosure, transfers the entire burden of vigilance to the busiest party in the arrangement.\nNone of this means that convenience should be refused. The point is that the trade is being conducted on terms nobody negotiated. A city that asks for a fingerprint to enter a swimming pool has made a judgement about what convenience is worth; residents were not consulted, and each of them, alone, can do very little about it. Privacy, in the end, is less a private preference than a collective condition, and conditions are set by rules rather than by clicks.","questions":[{"n":46,"stem":"What does the author suggest about the gap between what people say about privacy and what they do?","options":{"A":"It shows that most consumers are indifferent to how their data is used.","B":"It reflects a reasonable calculation rather than simple insincerity.","C":"It has been exaggerated by researchers who study online behaviour.","D":"It results mainly from a widespread ignorance of privacy law."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 1 段末两句把这一落差说成 a rational response to an unequal exchange，并否定 hypocrisy 的说法，B 项 reasonable calculation 即其同义改写。A 项与首句 most say yes, emphatically 矛盾；D 项把原因归于不懂法律，原文强调的是代价的时间差。"},{"n":47,"stem":"The word 'asymmetry' (Paragraph 2) refers to the difference between the two sides in terms of","options":{"A":"how soon the costs and benefits of sharing data are felt.","B":"how many services require data and how many do not.","C":"how strictly firms and consumers are regulated.","D":"how much younger and older users trust technology."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 2 段先讲 costs of refusing are immediate and the costs of consenting are deferred，再说 the benefit of protection is invisible and statistical，故 asymmetry 指代价与收益在时间与可见度上的不对等。C 项把不对称误解为监管力度差异，原文未提。"},{"n":48,"stem":"What does the author imply about consent notices in Paragraph 3?","options":{"A":"They offer a genuine choice to users who read them carefully.","B":"They are too brief to contain the information users need.","C":"They mainly serve to make data sharing look acceptable.","D":"They have been simplified after sustained public criticism."},"answer":"C","explanation":"答案 C。第 3 段末句 Consent was meant to be a brake; it has become a formality that legitimises the speed，说明同意程序已沦为替数据流动背书的仪式。A 项与 a form nobody reads 相悖；B 项与 Notices are long 正相反。"},{"n":49,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards setting minimum standards for certain categories of data?","options":{"A":"Supportive, since some decisions are too complex for individuals to make alone.","B":"Doubtful, because such standards would raise costs for technology firms.","C":"Neutral, as the evidence about their effects remains inconclusive.","D":"Critical, because they would narrow the choices open to consumers."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 4 段第三句说设置底线 does not insult consumers' intelligence; it acknowledges that some transactions are too complex for case-by-case negotiation，态度明确为赞同。D 项把『禁止某些交易』曲解为减少选择；B 项的成本问题原文并未讨论。"},{"n":50,"stem":"What does the author conclude about privacy at the end of the passage?","options":{"A":"It may properly be surrendered whenever the convenience gained is large.","B":"It depends on rules that bind everyone rather than on individual decisions.","C":"It can be safeguarded only if consumers read the terms they accept.","D":"It is a personal taste that public authorities cannot influence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。末段最后一句 Privacy is less a private preference than a collective condition, and conditions are set by rules rather than by clicks。C 项恰是作者论证已失效的路径；D 项与 collective condition 直接冲突。"}]},{"key":"C2","type":"careful_reading","name":"Section C 仔细阅读（Passage Two）","short":"仔细阅读二","instructions":"","passage":"The vocabulary of migration has not caught up with the climate. International law recognises refugees who flee persecution and grants them a defined status with rights attached. A family whose village has been submerged by a rising river, or whose wells have turned saline, fits none of the categories. They may be displaced, destitute and unable to return, and still be, in the eyes of the law, ordinary migrants who moved for reasons of their own choosing.\nThe consequence is a protection gap that is easy to describe and hard to close. Because climate displacement is rarely sudden, it produces no dramatic border crossing and no single persecutor to name. People leave in stages: a season of failed crops, a move to a nearby town, a second move to a city, eventually a journey across a border that looks, in the records, like an economic decision. Officials assessing such claims are not being cruel when they apply the existing tests; they are applying tests that were designed for a different problem.\nSome governments have experimented with new categories. Temporary protected status, humanitarian visas and bilateral labour agreements can all be stretched to cover climate-affected arrivals, and several have been. The difficulty is that each of these instruments is discretionary, revocable and tied to the mood of a particular government. A status granted by administrative grace can be withdrawn by the same grace, which leaves people building lives on ground that may open beneath them.\nA second difficulty is that the places people move to are often as exposed as the places they left. Urban districts that absorb rural arrivals frequently sit on flood plains or unstable slopes, and newcomers occupy the cheapest and most hazardous housing. Adaptation funding tends to flow towards capitals and towards visible infrastructure; the neighbourhoods where displacement actually lands are rarely consulted. Migration is thus treated as a problem to be managed at the border, when most of its consequences are felt in the city.\nWhat would a serious response look like? It would probably begin by admitting that the categories are administrative conveniences rather than descriptions of reality. It would fund the towns and districts that receive people, not only the agencies that count them. And it would accept that some movement is now unavoidable, which makes preparation cheaper than prevention and far cheaper than panic. The alternative is a legal system that continues to ask whether a person qualifies as a refugee while the ground they came from disappears.","questions":[{"n":51,"stem":"Why does the author say that the vocabulary of migration has not caught up with the climate?","options":{"A":"Because legal definitions of refuge do not cover people displaced by environmental change.","B":"Because treaties are revised too slowly to keep pace with rising temperatures.","C":"Because courts have refused to hear cases brought by climate scientists.","D":"Because governments cannot agree on what causes global warming."},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 1 段第二、三句说国际法只承认因迫害而逃的难民，而被淹的村庄或咸化的水井 fits none of the categories。B 项偷换为条约修订速度，原文谈的是类别不适用；C、D 两项原文均未提及。"},{"n":52,"stem":"What does the author suggest about officials who reject climate-related claims?","options":{"A":"They deliberately ignore evidence of environmental damage.","B":"They are applying rules written for a different situation.","C":"They have not been trained in environmental science.","D":"They act under pressure from bilateral labour agreements."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 2 段末句 Officials are not being cruel... they are applying tests that were designed for a different problem。A 项与 not being cruel 相反；C 项把制度问题说成培训不足，属过度推断。"},{"n":53,"stem":"The word 'discretionary' (Paragraph 3) is closest in meaning to","options":{"A":"dependent on official judgement","B":"limited to a fixed period","C":"open to public challenge","D":"shared among several states"},"answer":"A","explanation":"答案 A。第 3 段说这些工具 discretionary, revocable and tied to the mood of a particular government，随后 A status granted by administrative grace 进一步解释，可见指由官方自行裁量。B 项对应的是 revocable 之外的期限含义，原文未提。"},{"n":54,"stem":"What problem does the author identify with the places that receive climate migrants?","options":{"A":"They are usually located in wealthier and better protected regions.","B":"They often expose newcomers to hazards similar to those they fled.","C":"They receive most of the adaptation money available.","D":"They lack the labour force needed to absorb new arrivals."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。第 4 段首句 the places people move to are often as exposed as the places they left，并提到新来者住在最便宜也最危险的住房。C 项与 Adaptation funding tends to flow towards capitals 相反；D 项劳动力问题原文未涉及。"},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the main point of the passage?","options":{"A":"Climate migration should be halted through large-scale engineering works.","B":"The legal framework and the reality of climate displacement are badly matched.","C":"Economic migrants deserve exactly the same protection as refugees.","D":"Cities rather than national governments must take charge of the problem."},"answer":"B","explanation":"答案 B。全文从法律类别不适用（第 1、2 段）、临时措施不可靠（第 3 段）到接收地同样脆弱（第 4 段），都在说明制度与现实错位，末段 the categories are administrative conveniences 是总括。A 项与末段 preparation cheaper than prevention 相悖；D 项把『后果发生在城市』扩大为『应由城市负责』。"}]}],"writing":{"directions":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the pros and cons of starting a business right after graduation. You should discuss both sides and give your own view. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.","prompt_cn":"以「毕业后立即创业的利与弊」为题写一篇短文，讨论正反两方面并给出你自己的看法。","word_limit":150,"sample_essay":"Every spring, thousands of graduates weigh a job offer against a business idea, and the advice they receive is rarely neutral. Starting a company at twenty-two has real advantages. Energy is high, obligations are few, and a failure at that age costs little more than a year and some pride. The habits formed while solving a real problem for real customers are hard to acquire inside a large organisation, where responsibility is divided until it disappears.\nThe disadvantages are equally real. Most graduates have never managed a budget, hired anyone or lost a customer, and a business is an unforgiving classroom. Capital is scarce when credibility is thinnest, and investors prefer founders who have seen how a functioning company operates. There is also a hidden opportunity cost: the early years of a career are when professional judgement is formed.\nThe sensible conclusion is neither to rush nor to wait indefinitely. A graduate with a specific problem, some savings and a partner with complementary skills should probably try. The rest would do better to spend two or three years learning how organisations work, then start something with better questions.","sample_essay_cn":"每年春天，成千上万的毕业生都要在录用通知与创业想法之间权衡，而他们得到的建议很少是中立的。二十二岁创办公司确有实际优势：精力充沛，牵绊很少，这个年纪的失败，代价不过是一年和一点自尊。在为大客户解决真实问题的过程中养成的习惯，在大型机构里很难获得，因为那里的责任被层层分割，直到消失不见。\n劣势同样真实。多数毕业生从未管过预算、招过人，也没有失去过客户，而创业是一间不讲情面的课堂。资金恰在最缺信誉的时候最为紧缺，投资者也更偏爱见过一家公司如何运转的创始人。还有一项容易被忽视的机会成本：职业生涯的头几年，正是职业判断力成形的时期。\n明智的结论既不是急于行动，也不是无限期等待。如果一个毕业生有具体想解决的问题、有一些积蓄，还有一位技能互补的伙伴，那不妨一试。其余的人最好先花两三年弄清组织如何运作，然后再带着更好的问题去创业。"},"translation":{"source_cn":"在中国，手机付款早已是日常生活的常态。在超市、街边小摊和乡镇集市，顾客扫一下二维码，几秒就能完成交易，省去了找零的麻烦，也让许多小商贩第一次有了可查证的经营记录。近年来，数字支付扩展到公共交通和政务服务，人们可在同一应用里缴费预约。便利之外也有困扰：老年人不熟悉操作，消费记录被平台大量收集，手机丢失便寸步难行。因此，推广新技术的同时保留现金等替代方式，是城市管理者要认真对待的问题。","reference_en":"In China, paying by mobile phone has long been an ordinary part of daily life. In supermarkets, at street stalls and in township markets, a customer scans a code and completes a purchase in seconds, which saves the trouble of making change and has given many small traders something they never had before: a verifiable record of their business. In recent years digital payment has spread to public transport and government services, so that bills can be paid and appointments booked within a single application. Convenience brings its own troubles: elderly people find the procedures unfamiliar, spending records are collected by platforms on a large scale, and a lost phone can leave a person unable to pay for anything. How to promote new technology while keeping cash and other alternatives available is therefore a question city authorities need to take seriously.","vocab":[],"reference_en_ok":true}}};
